/* Gnamma Stage 84: provisional page-aligned source records; not a certified critical edition. */
window.__GNAMMA_SANANDAJI84.push(...[{"id":"I234.lower.01","volume":1,"page":234,"physical_pdf_page":247,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"لا يستلزم الجواز في نفس الأمر على ما هو المتنازع لجواز أن يمتنع لدليل خارجي\nكذا قيل","english":"[Reason’s finding no impossibility] does not entail possibility in reality, which is the disputed issue: an extrinsic proof may establish impossibility. So it has been said.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I233.lower.05","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I234.lower.02","volume":1,"page":234,"physical_pdf_page":247,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله الحاصل بسبب إحساس الجزئيات الخ) قال في شرح المقاصد\nما حاصله أن مناط التكليف هو العقل والمراد به هنا هو العلم ببعض الضروريات\nالكلية إذ لو كان غير العلم لصح انفكاكهما بأن يوجد عالم لا يعقل وعاقل لا يعلم\nوهو باطل وليس هو العلم بالنظريات لأنه مشروط بالعقل فيلزم تأخر الشيء عن نفسه\nولا العلم بجميع الضروريات وإلا لما صدق على من يفقد بعضها لفقد شرط من\nالتفات أو تجربة أو تواتر أو غير ذلك مع أنه عاقل اتفاقا واعترض بمنع الملازمة\nفإن المتغايرين قد يتلازمان وبمنع بطلان اللازم أيضا فإن العاقل قد يكون بلا علم","english":"On “Arising through sensation of particulars, etc.” The substance of what is said in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid is this: the basis of accountability is intellect. What is meant here is knowledge of some universal necessary truths. If it were other than knowledge, the two could be separated, so that there could be a knowledgeable person without intellect and an intelligent person without knowledge; that is false. It is not knowledge of theoretical matters, since such knowledge is conditioned by intellect, which would make a thing posterior to itself. Nor is it knowledge of all necessary truths: otherwise the description would not apply to someone lacking some of them because a condition is absent—attention, experience, mass transmission or another condition—although, by agreement, he is intelligent. The objection rejects the implication: distinct things may be inseparable. It also disputes the alleged falsity of the consequent, since an intelligent person may lack knowledge.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[],"proof_notes":["عاقل اتفاقا confirmed from enlarged original; remove editorial brackets around intelligent."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I234.upper.01","volume":1,"page":234,"physical_pdf_page":247,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"عن الصفة لا تجعلها متعددة متكثرة","english":"[The multiplicity of relations] external to the attribute does not make the attribute itself multiple or manifold.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I233.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I234.upper.02","volume":1,"page":234,"physical_pdf_page":247,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) الأدلة السمعية دلت على أن (محله)\nأي العلم الحادث (القلب) وإن لم يتعين هو لذلك عقلا بل يجوز أن يخلقه الله\nتعالى في أي جوهر شاء (الآن الكلام في القلب) والظاهر من كلام كثير من\nالمحققين أن ليس المراد بالقلب العضو المخصوص الموجود لكل حيوان بل الروح\nالذي به امتاز الإنسان (ولا كلام في توسط الآلات) أي المشاعر الظاهرة\nوالباطنة (في العلم بالجزئيات) وسيجيء إن شاء الله تعالى في باب الجواهر\nتفصيله","english":"“And” the transmitted proofs indicate that “its locus,” namely the locus of originated knowledge, is “the heart.” Reason does not, however, determine that locus exclusively: God may create knowledge in any substance He wills. “For now, the discussion concerns the heart.” The apparent meaning of many verifying scholars’ statements is that “heart” does not mean the particular bodily organ found in every animal, but the spirit by which the human being is distinguished. “There is no dispute about instruments’ mediation”—the external and internal senses—“in knowledge of particulars.” Its detailed account will come, God willing, in the chapter on substances.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"الآن الكلام في القلب","reading":"الآن الكلام في القلب","status":"visible wording; boundary and punctuation require caution"}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I234.upper.03","volume":1,"page":234,"physical_pdf_page":247,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ومناط التكليف) الشرعي (القوة الحاصلة عند العلم) الحاصل\nبسبب إحساس الجزئيات والتنبه للمشاركات والمباينات (ببعض المعلومات)\nالكلية (الضرورية) فإن النفس إذا أحست جزئيات كثيرة وارتسمت صورها\nفي آلاتها ولاحظت نسبة بعضها إلى بعض استعدت لأن يفيض عليها من المبدأ\nالفياض صور كلية وأحكام تصديقية فيما بينها فهذه علوم ضرورية (بحيث\n[موضع مطموس في الصورة الأصلية]\nمنها (وهي القوة المميزة بين الأمور الحسنة والقبيحة) حتى لا يتوجه التكليف\nعلى فاقد تمييزه من المجانين والصبيان والبهائم","english":"“The basis of” revealed “accountability is the faculty acquired when there is knowledge”—arising through sensation of particulars and awareness of their agreements and differences—“of certain” universal “necessary objects of knowledge.” For, when the soul senses many particulars, their forms are imprinted in its instruments, and it considers their relations to one another, it becomes prepared for universal forms and assertoric judgments concerning their relations to flow upon it from the overflowing principle. These are necessary cognitions, “such that [a line or lines are obscured in the supplied image] … from them.” “It is the faculty that distinguishes good and bad things,” so that accountability does not apply to those lacking discernment: the insane, children and beasts.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_gap":true,"editorial":"A white damaged band interrupts this upper passage. The bracket is an editorial description of the image, not Arabic supplied as source text. This page is excluded from all-register completion. No text has been reconstructed from doctrine or memory.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I234.upper.04","volume":1,"page":234,"physical_pdf_page":247,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ومنها) أي من الكيفيات النفسانية","english":"“Among them,” that is, among the psychological qualities—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I235.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I235.lower.01","volume":1,"page":235,"physical_pdf_page":248,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"في حقيقتهما وأجيب بأن التوقف إنما هو على تصورهما بوجه ما وهو لا يلزم أن\nيكون بالنظر والحق أن هذا نزاع لفظي يرجع إلى تفسير التصديق الضروري هل\nهو الذي لا يفتقر إلى النظر أصلا لا في نفسه ولا في أطرافه أو هو الذي لا يفتقر\nإليه في نفسه فقط كذا حرره بعض المحققين","english":"[Dependence concerns examining] their realities. The reply is that it depends only on conceiving them in some respect, which need not require inquiry. The truth is that this is a verbal disagreement turning on the explanation of necessary assent: is it that which needs no inquiry at all, either in itself or in its terms, or that which needs no inquiry only in itself? This is how one of the verifying scholars formulated it.","reading_status":"first_pass","duplicate_of":"I233.lower.03","editorial":"The opening is partly damaged. The same passage is clearly visible in the lower register of I:233. This is a repeated visible text, not new content or an additional completed argument.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I235.lower.02","volume":1,"page":235,"physical_pdf_page":248,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله الحادث الخ) بخلاف القديم\nفإنهم اتفقوا على أنه واحد يتعلق بمعلومات متعددة وأما الحادث فقد اختلفوا فيه\nفذهب الشيخ ومن تبعه إلى أن الواحد منه يمتنع أن يتعلق بمعلومين مثلا وهو\nالمعنى بقولهم تعدد العلم بتعدد المعلوم وقد يقال لو تعلق الواحد منه بما فوق الواحد\nلزم تعلقه بما لا نهاية له ولا يخفى ضعفه جدا وذهب بعض الأصحاب إلى أنه يجوز\nبل يجب واستدل عليه بأن العلم بالشيء يستلزم العلم بذلك وهكذا لا إلى نهاية\nفلو لم تكن عدة من هذه المعلومات معلومة بعلم واحد لزم حصول علوم غير متناهية\nلكل من علم شيئا واحدا ولا يخفى ضعف هذا أيضا","english":"On “Originated, etc.” Unlike eternal knowledge: they agree that it is one and relates to many objects of knowledge. They disagree, however, about originated knowledge. The Shaykh and his followers held that a single instance cannot relate, for example, to two objects. This is what is meant by their saying that knowledge is multiplied with the multiplication of its objects. It may be argued that if a single instance could relate to more than one object, it would have to relate to infinitely many; the extreme weakness of this argument is evident. Some of our companions held that such a relation is possible, indeed necessary. They argued that knowing a thing entails knowing that one knows it, and so on without end. If several of these objects were not known through one cognition, every person knowing a single thing would possess infinitely many cognitions. The weakness of this argument too is evident.","reading_status":"first_pass","duplicate_of":"I233.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I235.lower.03","volume":1,"page":235,"physical_pdf_page":248,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أو صفة ذات إضافة الخ)\nأورد عليه أن عدم الامتناع عند العقل بالنظر إلى كون العلم صفة ذات إضافة","english":"On “Or an attribute possessing a relation, etc.” The objection was raised that reason’s finding no impossibility, in view of knowledge’s being an attribute possessing a relation—","reading_status":"first_pass","duplicate_of":"I233.lower.05","editorial":"This repeated fragment is not completed by the beginning of I:236’s lower register. Its continuation is at I:234 lower. This physical-text anomaly remains an explicit source issue.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I235.upper.01","volume":1,"page":235,"physical_pdf_page":248,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(الإرادة وهي كسائر الوجدانيات) في أنه (يسهل معرفتها) ومعناها واضح\nعند العقل غير ملتبس بغيرها (ويعسر تعريفها) بكنه الحقيقة ولهذا اختلف\nفي تفسيرها","english":"“Will, which is like other immediately experienced states” in that “it is easy to recognise”: its meaning is clear to the intellect and is not confused with other things, “but difficult to define” in the full depth of its reality. Hence the disagreement over its explanation.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I234.upper.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I235.upper.02","volume":1,"page":235,"physical_pdf_page":248,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وتفارق الشهوة) التي هي توقان النفس نحو الأمور المستلذة (في\nالوجود) فإن الإنسان قد يريد شرب دواء كريه ولا يشتهيه بل ينفر عنه وقد\nيشتهي الطعام اللذيذ ولا يريده إذا علم أن فيه هلاكه (ولشدة تعلقها) أي\nالإرادة (بالقوة المدركة) تعلق (الشهوة بالطبيعة) فهذا أيضا يدل\nعلى تغايرهما","english":"“It differs from appetite”—the soul’s yearning towards pleasurable things—“in existence.” A person may will to drink an unpleasant medicine without desiring it, indeed while recoiling from it; and may desire delicious food without willing it, if he knows that it would destroy him. “And because of its close connection”—will’s connection—“with the apprehensive faculty,” whereas “appetite is connected with nature,” this too indicates that they are different.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The argument distinguishes will and appetite both by their possible separation and by the faculties or principles with which they are associated.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I235.upper.03","volume":1,"page":235,"physical_pdf_page":248,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(قيل) في تفسير الإرادة (هي اعتقاد النفع) والقائل كثير من\nالمعتزلة لأن نسبة القدرة إلى طرفي الفعل على السواء فإذا حصل اعتقاد النفع في\nأحد طرفيه ترجح على الآخر عند القادر وأثرت فيه قدرته (أو ميل يعقب ذلك)\nالاعتقاد وإليه ذهب بعضهم لأن القادر كثيرا ما يعتقد النفع ولا يريد ما لم يحدث\nهذا الميل","english":"“It has been said,” in explanation of will, “that it is belief in benefit.” Many Muʿtazilīs held this, because power relates equally to the two alternatives concerning an act. Once belief in benefit attaches to one alternative, it predominates over the other for the capable agent, whose power then produces its effect in it. “Or” will is “an inclination following that” belief. Some adopted this view because a capable agent often believes in benefit but does not will until this inclination occurs.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I235.upper.04","volume":1,"page":235,"physical_pdf_page":248,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(أو العلم بما هو عند العالم كمال وخير) كما هو مذهب الفلاسفة فإنهم\nلما ذهبوا إلى أنه تعالى موجب بالذات لا فاعل بالاختيار وعلى أن في الإرادة\n[عبارة آخر السطر/وصل الصفحة معيبة في الصورة]","english":"“Or” will is “knowledge of what, in the knower’s judgment, is perfection and good,” as is the philosophers’ view. For, since they held that God is a cause necessitating through His essence, not an agent through choice—[the wording at the end of the upper register is defective or incomplete in the supplied image]—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I236.upper.01","source_gap":true,"editorial":"The last visible phrase is not a secure grammatical join with I:236. No missing words are supplied silently. I:235 is excluded from completion pending a second copy. The lower portion of this page also visibly repeats material from I:233.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I236.lower.01","volume":1,"page":236,"physical_pdf_page":249,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ما يناسب الشيء من حيث اقتضاؤه [وإبرازه؟] ذلك الشيء من القوة إلى الفعل كمال له ومن\nحيث كونه مؤثرا خير","english":"What suits a thing, insofar as it requires it and [brings] that thing from potentiality into actuality, is a perfection for it; insofar as it produces an effect, it is good.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"not_contiguous_with_I235_lower","editorial":"This is printed at the beginning of the lower register but does not continue the duplicated knowledge-gloss fragment on I:235. The broken sequence is recorded, not silently repaired.","readings":[{"locus":"after اقتضاؤه","reading":"وإبرازه","status":"Probable reading of the compressed word, not externally confirmed. The English marks the uncertain verb."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I236.lower.02","volume":1,"page":236,"physical_pdf_page":249,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لأن الحركة الإرادية الخ) والجواب أن المراد بالإرادة\nالمشتركة بين الحيوانات حالة ميلانية إلى الفعل أو الترك وهي منتفية عن الواجب","english":"On “Since voluntary motion, etc.” The reply is that the will shared by animals means a state of inclination towards acting or refraining, and this is absent from the Necessary Existent.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I236.lower.03","volume":1,"page":236,"physical_pdf_page":249,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولا يتوقف على ترجيح الخ) قالوا هذا التفسير كما لا يقتضي كونها من جنس\nالاعتقاد والميل لا يقتضي كون متعلقها مقدورا لجواز أن يكون صفة تتعلق\nبالمقدور وغيره ويكون من شأنها الترجيح لأحد طرفي المقدور ولهذا جاز إرادة المحال\nوالموت بخلاف ما قيل أن متعلق الإرادة على هذا التفسير لا يكون إلا مقدورا","english":"On “Without depending on preference, etc.” They said: just as this explanation does not require will to belong to the genus of belief or inclination, it does not require its object to be within power. It may be an attribute relating both to what is within power and to what is not, while being of a nature to prefer one of the two alternatives concerning what is within power. Thus willing an impossibility or death is possible, contrary to the claim that, on this explanation, the object of will can only be something within power—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I237.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I236.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":236,"physical_pdf_page":249,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله قلنا هي نسبتها الخ) وقلنا إنها لذاتها تقتضي التعلق بما هو راجح عند الفاعل\nولا إيجاب لأن الفاعل الموجب لا يختلف فعله بخلاف المختار لأنه قد يكون الراجح\nعنده الفعل وقد يكون الترك وذلك لاختلاف المصالح بحسب اختلاف الأوقات\nاه منه","english":"On “We reply: its relation, etc.” We also say that by its very nature it requires attachment to what is preferable in the agent’s judgment. No necessitation follows, since the action of a necessitating agent does not vary, unlike that of one who chooses: what is preferable in his judgment may be acting, or it may be refraining, owing to differences of benefit with differing times. End. From him.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[],"editorial":"The printed منه is retained without identifying a personal author. This small ruled note is independent of the long lower gloss.","proof_notes":["عنده الفعل confirmed: the act may be preferable in the voluntary agent’s estimation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I236.upper.01","volume":1,"page":236,"physical_pdf_page":249,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"لاختصت بذوي العلم وليست كذلك لأن الحركة الإرادية مأخوذة في تعريف\nمطلق الحيوان","english":"[Were will explained in this way,] it would be restricted to those possessing knowledge. It is not, since voluntary motion is included in the definition of animal in general.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I235.upper.04","editorial":"The bracket gives only the contextual conditional required by the surviving clause. It is not a reconstruction of the lost Arabic at the preceding page boundary.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I236.upper.02","volume":1,"page":236,"physical_pdf_page":249,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والتفسير) لها (بصفة بها يرجح الفاعل أحد مقدوريه من\nالفعل وتركه) كما ذهب أصحابنا لأنها غير مشروطة باعتقاد النفع أو ميل\nيعقبه فإن الهارب إذا عن له طريقان متساويان في النجاة فيختار أحدهما بالإرادة\nولا يتوقف على ترجيح أحدهما للنفع يعتقده فيه أو ميل يعقبه (لا يكشف)\nهذا التفسير أيضا (عن حقيقتها) لأنا لا نسلم وجود صفة كذلك فما أن\nتساوت نسبتها إلى الطرفين احتيج في التعلق بأحدهما إلى مخصص لامتناع\nالترجيح بلا مرجح وينقل الكلام إليه فيلزم الدور والتسلسل أو لا فيلزم الإيجاب\nقلنا هي نسبتها إلى الطرفين سواء لكنها لكونها تابعة للإدراك يتعلقها الفاعل بما","english":"“Explaining” it “as an attribute through which the agent gives preference to one of the two things within his power—acting and refraining”—as our companions hold, since it is not conditioned by belief in benefit or by an inclination following such belief: a fugitive who encounters two paths equally offering escape chooses one by will, without depending on his preference for a benefit he believes it offers or on a subsequent inclination—this explanation too “does not disclose its reality.” For [the objection runs] we do not concede the existence of such an attribute. Either it relates equally to both alternatives, in which case its attachment to one requires a specifying factor, since preference without a preferrer is impossible; the same question then applies to that factor, entailing circularity or an infinite regress. Or it does not relate equally, in which case necessitation follows. We reply: its relation to both alternatives is equal, but because it follows apprehension, the agent directs it to what—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I237.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"للنفع يعتقده فيه","reading":"لنفع يعتقده فيه","status":"enlargement to confirm preposition"},{"locus":"فما أن","reading":"فإما أن","status":"normalised reading to confirm"}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I237.lower.01","volume":1,"page":237,"physical_pdf_page":250,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"فيمتنع تعلقها بالإرادة كما مر آنفا قلنا نسبتها إلى الطرفين يعني أن ترجيحها لأحد\nالطرفين محض نفسها من غير حاجة إلى رجحان واعتقاد نفع في ذلك الطرف فتدبر","english":"[The preceding account makes] its relation to will impossible, as was just explained. We reply: its relation to the two alternatives means that its preference for one of them proceeds simply from itself, without needing that alternative’s preponderance or a belief that benefit lies in it. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I236.lower.03","readings":[],"proof_notes":["The adversarial transition is قلنا, “we reply”, not فلما."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I237.lower.02","volume":1,"page":237,"physical_pdf_page":250,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ثم لا يخفى الخ) أقول لا يجوز أن يكون مراد الأشعري هو أنه إذا تحقق\nالشعور بالشيء وضده يتحد إرادة أحدهما بكراهة الآخر فتدبر ثم اعلم أن القائلين بالتغاير\nاختلفوا في الاستلزام فذهب الغزالي إلى أن إرادة الشيء تستلزم كراهة ضده المشعور\nبه إذ لو لم يكن مكروها لكان مرادا فيلزم إرادة الضدين وهو محال لأن الإرادتين\nالمتعلقتين بالضدين متضادتان وأجيب بمنع المقدمتين أما الأولى فلجواز أن لا يتعلق\nكراهة ولا إرادة بالضد ككثير من الأمور المشعور بها وأما الثانية فلجواز أن يكون\nكل من الضدين مرادا من وجه غير وجه الآخر","english":"On “Moreover, it is evident, etc.” I say: could al-Ashʿarī not mean that, when awareness of a thing and its contrary is present, willing one is identical with disliking the other? Reflect. Know, further, that those who distinguish the two differ over implication. Al-Ghazālī held that willing a thing entails disliking its contrary when that contrary is apprehended. For, were it not disliked, it would be willed; this would entail willing two contraries, which is impossible because two volitions directed to contraries are themselves contrary. The reply rejects both premises. As to the first, the contrary may be the object of neither dislike nor will, as is the case with many apprehended things. As to the second, each contrary may be willed in a respect different from that in which the other is willed.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"لا يجوز أن يكون is read interrogatively here: the annotator proposes a restricted interpretation that would answer the preceding objection, rather than denying it.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I237.lower.03","volume":1,"page":237,"physical_pdf_page":250,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولكن لم تؤثر الخ) والنزاع","english":"On “But has not produced the effect, etc.” The disagreement—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I238.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I237.upper.01","volume":1,"page":237,"physical_pdf_page":250,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"هو راجح عنده","english":"[That alternative] is preferable in his judgment.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I236.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I237.upper.02","volume":1,"page":237,"physical_pdf_page":250,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وزعم الأشعري أن إرادة الشيء نفس كراهة ضده) إذ لو كانت\nمثلها أو ضدها لما جاز اجتماعهما ولو كانت مخالفة لها لجاز اجتماع كل منهما مع\nضد الآخر كالسواد المخالف للحلاوة يجتمع مع ضدها الذي هو الحموضة فلزم\nجواز اجتماع إرادة الشيء مع إرادة الضد لأن ضد كراهة الضد نفس إرادة الضد\nوأجيب بأنا لا نسلم لزوم جواز اجتماع كل من المختلفين مع ضد الآخر لجواز أن\nيكونا متلازمين ويمتنع اجتماع الملزوم مع ضد اللازم ثم لا يخفى أنه ربما أراد الشيء\nولا يخطر بالبال ضده فضلا عن أن يكرهه","english":"“Al-Ashʿarī maintained that willing a thing is identical with disliking its contrary.” For if it were of the same kind as that dislike, or contrary to it, the two could not coexist; and if it were different from it, each could coexist with the contrary of the other. For example, blackness, which differs from sweetness, can coexist with sweetness’s contrary, sourness. It would then follow that willing a thing could coexist with willing its contrary, because the contrary of disliking the contrary is precisely willing that contrary. The reply is that we do not concede that, of any two different things, each must be capable of coexisting with the contrary of the other. They may be mutually implicative, and the entailing item cannot coexist with the contrary of what it entails. Moreover, it is evident that someone may will a thing without its contrary occurring to him at all, let alone disliking it.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"مثلها, “of the same kind as it,” concerns the alleged impossibility of two similar accidents in one subject, not numerical identity. The argument is reported, followed by its rebuttal.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I237.upper.03","volume":1,"page":237,"physical_pdf_page":250,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ومنها القدرة وهي صفة تؤثر وفق الإرادة)\nخرج ما لا يؤثر كالعلم إذ لا تأثير له وإن توقف تأثير القدرة عليه وما يؤثر لا على وفق\nالإرادة كالقوى النباتية والعنصرية (أو) هي (مبدأ الأفعال المختلفة) [كقيل؟]\nفإن قيل القدرة الحادثة غير مؤثرة عندنا فلا تدخل في شيء من التعريفين قلنا\nليس المراد التأثير بالفعل بل بالقوة بمعنى أنها صفة من شأنها التأثير والإيجاد والقدرة\nالحادثة كذلك ولكن لم تؤثر لوقوع متعلقها بقدرة الله تعالى","english":"“Among them is power: an attribute that produces an effect in accordance with the will.” This excludes what produces no effect, such as knowledge—for knowledge itself has no efficacy, although the efficacy of power depends on it—and what produces an effect not in accordance with the will, such as the vegetative and elemental faculties. “Or” power is “the principle of different acts,” [as has been said]. If it is objected that, on our account, originated power has no efficacy and therefore falls under neither definition, we reply: actual efficacy is not intended, but potential efficacy, in the sense that this attribute is of a nature to produce effects and bring things into existence. Originated power is such, but has not produced the effect, because its object occurs through God’s power.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"after المختلفة","reading":"كقيل","status":"Unclear short connective in the source. “As has been said” is a marked contextual reading, not a verified expanded Arabic phrase."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I237.upper.04","volume":1,"page":237,"physical_pdf_page":250,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والقوة أعم)\nمن القدرة بكلا المعنيين (إذ) هي صفة (هي مبدأ التغيير في آخر من حيث","english":"“Quwwa is more general” than power in either sense, “since” it is an attribute “that is the principle of change in another insofar as—”","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I238.upper.01","editorial":"Quwwa is retained in this semantic discussion because the author distinguishes its ordinary sense, strength, from successively broadened technical senses.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I238.lower.01","volume":1,"page":238,"physical_pdf_page":251,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"في أنها هل هي تسمى قدرة بدون التأثير بالفعل أو لا نزاع في اللفظ والتسمية فتدبر","english":"[The disagreement] over whether it is called power without actually producing an effect is a disagreement over the word and designation. Reflect.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I237.lower.03","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I238.lower.02","volume":1,"page":238,"physical_pdf_page":251,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله قيد الحيثية الخ) أي بعد اعتبار قيد الآخرية المفيد لوجوب التغاير بين\nالمؤثر والمتأثر","english":"On “The qualification ‘insofar as,’ etc.” That is, after taking into account the qualification of otherness, which entails a distinction between what affects and what is affected.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I238.lower.03","volume":1,"page":238,"physical_pdf_page":251,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله التغاير الاعتباري الخ) مثل بالطبيب المعالج لنفسه واعترض\nبأن التغاير فيه حقيقي لأن التأثير للنفس والتأثر للبدن فالأقرب التمثيل بالمعالج\nنفسه في تهذيب الأخلاق وتبديل الملكات","english":"On “A distinction in consideration, etc.” The example given is a physician treating himself. The objection is that the distinction here is real: the soul is what acts, while the body is what is affected. A better example would therefore be a person treating himself by refining his character and altering his dispositions.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I238.lower.04","volume":1,"page":238,"physical_pdf_page":251,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله معناه المتعارف الخ) قال في شرح\nالمقاصد لفظ القوة يقال لصفة يتمكن الحيوان بها من مزاولة أفعال شاقة ويقابلها\nالضعف انتهى فتدبر","english":"On “Its ordinary meaning, etc.” In Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid he says: the word quwwa is applied to an attribute through which an animal is able to undertake arduous acts; its opposite is weakness. End of quotation. Reflect.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I238.lower.05","volume":1,"page":238,"physical_pdf_page":251,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إلى سببه المسمى قدرة الخ) وأما بالنظر إلى ما في شرح\nالمقاصد المذكور آنفا فإلى أعم منه","english":"On “To its cause, called power, etc.” In relation to the passage from Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid just cited, however, the transfer is to something more general than it.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I238.lower.06","volume":1,"page":238,"physical_pdf_page":251,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وإلى لازمه الخ) أي لازم المعنى المتعارف","english":"On “And to its concomitant, etc.” That is, the concomitant of the ordinary meaning.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I238.lower.07","volume":1,"page":238,"physical_pdf_page":251,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بالنسبة إلى الفعل) وأما بالنسبة إلى الفاعل فهي تمكنه من تحصيل\nالشيء مع عدمه","english":"On “In relation to the act.” In relation to the agent, however, it is his ability to bring about the thing while the thing is absent.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I238.lower.08","volume":1,"page":238,"physical_pdf_page":251,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله من الصفة المؤثرة الخ) أي أعم من أن يكون تأثيرها بالإرادة\nأو لا فهذا تعميم في سبب المعنى المتعارف على وزان التعميم الذي في لازم ذلك المعنى\nالمبين بقوله ثم عمم الخ وأنت خبير بأن فيه اضطرابا فالأولى أن يقال معناها","english":"On “Of the efficacious attribute, etc.” That is, whether its efficacy is by will or not. This is therefore a generalisation concerning the cause of the ordinary meaning, corresponding to the generalisation concerning the concomitant of that meaning indicated by “It was then generalised, etc.” You are aware, however, that the account is confused. It would be better to say: its meaning—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I239.lower.01","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I238.upper.01","volume":1,"page":238,"physical_pdf_page":251,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"هو آخر) قيد الحيثية للإشعار بأنه يكفي التغاير الاعتباري قال بعض الأفاضل\nلفظ القوة معناه المتعارف عند الجمهور هو أن يتمكن الحي من الأفعال الشاقة\nثم نقل منه إلى سببه المسمى قدرة وهي صفة بها يتمكن الحي من الفعل وتركه\nبالإرادة وإلى لازمه أيضا وهو كونه بحيث لا ينفعل سريعا ثم عمم فاستعمل في كون\nالشيء مطلقا حيوانا كان أو غيره بهذه الحيثية ثم نقل من القدرة إلى لازمها بالنسبة\nإلى الفعل المقدور وهو إمكان حصوله مع عدمه وهذا الذي مقابل للفعل بمعنى\nالحصول وهو المتعارف بين المنطقيين وقد نقل أيضا من القدرة إلى ما هو كالجنس\nلها من الصفة المؤثرة المشتركة بينها وبين الإيجاد والقوة بهذا المعنى هي مبدأ التغيير","english":"“It is another.” The qualification “insofar as” indicates that a distinction in consideration is sufficient. One of the distinguished scholars said: in ordinary usage among people, the word quwwa means a living being’s ability to perform arduous acts. It was then transferred from this meaning to its cause, called power: an attribute by which a living being can act and refrain by will. It was also transferred to its concomitant, namely being such as not to be affected quickly. It was then generalised and used for a thing’s having this feature without restriction, whether an animal or something else. It was further transferred from power to its concomitant in relation to the possible act: the possibility of that act’s occurring while it is absent. This is what is opposed to actuality in the sense of occurrence, and is the familiar usage among logicians. It was also transferred from power to what is like a genus of power: the efficacious attribute shared by power and bringing-into-existence. Quwwa in this sense is the principle of change.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I237.upper.04","editorial":"This paragraph reports a sequence of semantic transfers, not one univocal definition of quwwa. “Another or otherwise” is retained as the wording of the text; the gloss supplies critical discussion of the shift in scope.","proof_notes":["Corrected حيوانا كان أو غيره: the generalisation is beyond animals, not beyond relative otherness.","Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I238.upper.02","volume":1,"page":238,"physical_pdf_page":251,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(إما مع القصد أو بدونه وكل) منها (إما مختلفة الآثار) والأفعال (أو لا)\n(فالأولى) وهي الصفة المؤثرة مع القصد والشعور واختلاف الآثار (القوة\nالحيوانية) التي يقال لها القدرة على التفسيرين (والثانية) وهي القوة المؤثرة","english":"[It is] “either accompanied by intention or without it; each” of these is “either productive of different effects” and acts “or not.” “The first,” the efficacious attribute accompanied by intention and awareness and productive of different effects, is “the animal faculty,” called power under both explanations. “The second,” the efficacious faculty—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I239.upper.01","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I239.lower.01","volume":1,"page":239,"physical_pdf_page":252,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"المتعارف ما مر آنفا من شرح المقاصد ثم عممت بنقلها إلى القدرة المذكورة ثم\nعممت بنقلها من القدرة إلى هذا المعنى أعني مبدأ التغيير فتأمل","english":"[It would be better to say:] its ordinary meaning is what was just quoted from Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid. It was then generalised by being transferred to the power described above, and then further generalised by transfer from power to this meaning, namely the principle of change. Consider this.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I238.lower.08","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I239.lower.02","volume":1,"page":239,"physical_pdf_page":252,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله على التفسير\nالثاني الخ) ظهر أن بين التفسيرين عموما من وجه","english":"On “Under the second explanation, etc.” It is now clear that the two explanations overlap without either being wholly included in the other.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I239.lower.03","volume":1,"page":239,"physical_pdf_page":252,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والمعلول بدون العلة\nالخ) هذا كلام الرازي على من يقول بتأثير القدرة الحادثة ثم أقول لعل مرادهم\nبكون القدرة مع الفعل لا قبله هو أن الفعل لابد أن يتعلق بالقدرة عند حصوله\nضرورة فلا يصح كون القدرة متقدمة عليه بحيث يكون الفعل حين حصوله غير مقدور\nوحين عدمه مقدورا فإنه ظاهر الفساد يدل على ذلك ما تمسكوا به أيضا على مدعاهم\nمن أن الفعل حال عدمه ممتنع كما بين ولا شيء من الممتنع بمقدور إذا تقرر هذا\nفالاستدلال المذكور بقولهم لامتناع بقاء الأعراض زمانين والرد عليهم بجواز الاستمرار\nبتجدد الأمثال لا يلائم هذا التحرير بل الرد لا يضر القائل بما ذكر ويصرح بما ذكرنا\nاحتجاج المعتزلة الآتي نقضا عليهم بلزوم إيجاد الموجود فإنه ناظر إلى كون الفعل حين","english":"On “And the effect without its cause, etc.” This is al-Rāzī’s argument against someone who holds that originated power has efficacy. I further say: perhaps what they intend by saying that power accompanies the act and does not precede it is that, when the act occurs, it must necessarily be related to power. Power therefore cannot precede it in such a way that the act is not an object of power when it occurs but is an object of power when absent; that is plainly untenable. This is indicated by their additional argument for the claim: an act, while absent, is impossible, as has been explained, and nothing impossible is an object of power. Once this is understood, the argument expressed by “because accidents cannot persist through two times,” and the reply that they may continue through the renewal of like instances, do not fit this formulation. Indeed, that reply does not harm the proponent of the position just set out. What we have said is made explicit by the Muʿtazilī argument that follows, objecting that their view entails bringing an already existent thing into existence; it considers the act at the time—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I240.lower.01","editorial":"The annotator’s reconstruction differs from the temporal-persistence argument in the upper text. Both are retained; the editorial voice does not reconcile them into a single proof.","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I239.upper.01","volume":1,"page":239,"physical_pdf_page":252,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"مع القصد والشعور ولكن على نهج واحد هي القوة (الفلكية) المسماة بالقدرة\nعلى التفسير الأول (والثالثة) وهي مبدأ الآثار المختلفة لا على سبيل القصد\nوالشعور القوة (النباتية) المسماة بالقدرة على التفسير الثاني (والرابعة)\nالتي هي مبدأ الآثار على نهج واحد دون القصد والشعور هي القوة (العنصرية)\nالتي ليست قدرة على شيء من التفسيرين","english":"[The second faculty acts] with intention and awareness, but according to a single pattern: it is the “celestial” faculty, called power under the first explanation. “The third,” the principle of different effects without intention and awareness, is the “vegetative” faculty, called power under the second explanation. “The fourth,” the principle of effects according to a single pattern without intention and awareness, is the “elemental” faculty, which is not power under either explanation.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I238.upper.02","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I239.upper.02","volume":1,"page":239,"physical_pdf_page":252,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ثم القدرة الحادثة) إنما توجد (مع الفعل\nلا قبله) إذ لو كانت قبله لانعدمت (لامتناع بقاء الأعراض) فيلزم وجود\nالمقدور بدون القدرة والمعلول بدون العلة وهو محال ولا يرد النقض بالقدرة القديمة\nلأنها ليست من قبيل الأعراض (ورد) بعد تسليم امتناع بقاء الأعراض (بأنها)\nيجوز أن (تستمر بتجدد الأمثال) إلى حال الفعل (كالعلم وغيره مما هو قبل الفعل)\nوفيه أن وجود المقدور حينئذ إن كان بالقدرة الزائلة يعود المحذور أو الحاصلة فهو\nالمطلوب","english":"“Originated power,” then, exists “together with the act, not before it.” For if it existed before the act it would cease to exist, “because accidents cannot persist.” The object of power would consequently exist without the power, and the effect without its cause, which is impossible. The counterexample of eternal power does not apply, because eternal power is not an accident. “The reply,” even conceding that accidents cannot persist, “is that it” may “continue through the renewal of like instances” until the act occurs, “like knowledge and other things that precede the act.” But if the object of power then exists through the power that has ceased to exist, the objection returns; if it exists through the power now present, that is precisely what was to be established.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I239.upper.03","volume":1,"page":239,"physical_pdf_page":252,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(قالوا) أي المعتزلة (لو لم تكن القدرة) إلا مع الفعل لم تتعلق (إلا حال\nوجود) (الفعل) ولو لم تتعلق إلا حال وجوده ومعنى تعلق القدرة به إيجاده (لزم إيجاد","english":"“They,” that is, the Muʿtazila, “said: if power existed” only together with the act, it would relate to it only “while” the act “exists.” If its relation were confined to the time of the act’s existence, and its relation to the act means bringing it into existence, “this would entail bringing into existence—”","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I240.upper.01","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I240.lower.01","volume":1,"page":240,"physical_pdf_page":253,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وجوده مقدورا ويلزم امتناع التكليف فإنه ناظر إلى كونه حين العدم غير مقدور\nوحينئذ فالجواب المناسب هو ما ذكره في شرح المقاصد بعد النقض بالقدرة القديمة\nمن أنه إن أرادوا امتناع الفعل حين العدم امتناعه مع وصف كونه معدوما فمسلم\nلكنه لا ينافي المقدورية وإمكان الحصول من القادر وإن أرادوا امتناعه في زمان\nعدمه فغير مسلم بل هو ممكن اللهم إلا أن يحرر المدعى بما يلائمه كما حرره الشارح\nقدس سره مد ظله","english":"[The preceding argument considers] the act’s being an object of power at the time it exists. [The argument that] imposing an obligation would become impossible considers its not being an object of power while absent. The appropriate reply is therefore what is said in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, after the counterexample of eternal power: if by the act’s impossibility while absent they mean its impossibility together with its being qualified as absent, that is conceded, but it does not conflict with its being an object of power and its possible occurrence through a capable agent. If they mean its impossibility during the time of its absence, that is not conceded: it is possible. Unless, that is, the claim is reformulated in a way appropriate to it, as the commentator—may his secret be sanctified; may his shade endure—has formulated it.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I239.lower.03","readings":[],"editorial":"Both printed honorifics are translated rather than used to infer the annotator’s identity.","proof_notes":["Enlargement establishes يحرر المدعى, “the claim is formulated”.","Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I240.lower.02","volume":1,"page":240,"physical_pdf_page":253,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولا يلزم أن يكون مقدورا حال الخ) هذا إذا اشترط أن يكون\nالمكلف به مقدورا بالفعل وأما إذا كانت مقدوريته بالقوة كافية لتكليفه به فلا نسلم\nأنه ليس مقدورا حال التكليف فإنه بالقوة لا بالفعل فظهر أن مرادهم من نفي\nالقدرة قبل الفعل نفي تعلقها به بالفعل لا بالقوة ومما قررنا ظهر لك ما للجواب\nالمناسب أيضا فتفطن فإن تحرير المقام ما قررناه","english":"On “The act need not be within power at the time, etc.” This applies if the object of obligation must actually be within power. But if its being potentially within power suffices for imposing the obligation, we do not concede that it is not within power at the time of obligation: it is within power potentially, not actually. Thus their denial of power before the act means denying its actual relation to the act, not its potential relation. From our explanation, the position of the appropriate reply also becomes evident. Attend carefully: the correct formulation of the issue is what we have explained.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","readings":[],"proof_notes":["Enlargement establishes بالقوة لا بالفعل, “potentially, not actually”.","Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I240.lower.03","volume":1,"page":240,"physical_pdf_page":253,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إذ لا فعل حينئذ فلا قدرة\nعليه الخ) الظاهر المسلم لما حررناه هو أن مقصودهم من ذلك هو أن الممنوع","english":"On “There is then no act, and therefore no power over it, etc.” The apparent interpretation that is acceptable in light of our formulation is that what they mean is that the person prevented—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I241.lower.01","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I240.upper.01","volume":1,"page":240,"physical_pdf_page":253,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الموجود) وتحصيل الحاصل (و) لزم أيضا (امتناع التكليف) لأن المكلف به\nإنما يكون قبل حصوله ضرورة أنه لا معنى لطلب حصول الحاصل وإذا كان الفعل قبل\nالوقوع غير مقدور كان جميع التكاليف الواقعة تكليفا بما لا يطاق وهو باطل\nبالاتفاق","english":"[This entails bringing] “an already existent thing” into existence and obtaining what has already been obtained. “And” it also entails “the impossibility of imposing an obligation,” because the object of obligation is [required] before it occurs: necessarily, there is no sense in demanding the occurrence of what has already occurred. If the act is not within power before it occurs, all actual obligations would impose what cannot be done. This is unanimously rejected.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I239.upper.03","readings":[],"proof_notes":["Enlargement establishes إنما; the restriction is retained in the English.","Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I240.upper.02","volume":1,"page":240,"physical_pdf_page":253,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ورد) الأول بأنه مبني على تأثير القدرة الحادثة وهو ممنوع ولئن سلم\nفـ(ما سبق) ذكره في الباب الثاني من أن إيجاد الموجود بالوجود الذي هو أثر ذلك الإيجاد غير مستحيل وإنما الممتنع إيجاد الموجود بوجود سابق","english":"“The reply” to the first argument is that it presupposes the efficacy of originated power, which is not conceded. Even if it is conceded, [the answer is] “what preceded” in the second chapter: it is not impossible to bring into existence something whose existence is the effect of that very bringing-into-existence. What is impossible is to bring into existence something that already possesses a prior existence.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","readings":[],"proof_notes":["The Arabic is بالوجود: existence which is the effect of this bringing-into-existence.","Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I240.upper.03","volume":1,"page":240,"physical_pdf_page":253,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) الثاني\n(بأنه يكفي في التكليف) عند من يقول بكون القدرة مع الفعل (كون الفعل\nمما يتعلق به القدرة في الجملة) ولا يلزم أن يكون مقدورا حال التكليف (كإيمان\nالكافر خلافا لخلق الجسم) ونحوه مما لا يتعلق به القدرة للعبد أصلا","english":"“And” the reply to the second is “that, for imposing an obligation, it suffices,” according to those who hold that power accompanies the act, “that the act be something to which power relates in some manner.” The act need not be within power at the time the obligation is imposed, “as with the unbeliever’s faith, unlike creating a body” and similar things to which the servant’s power does not relate at all.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","readings":[],"proof_notes":["Native page image confirms لخلق الجسم; the missing upper dot was a transcription error, not a variant.","Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I240.upper.04","volume":1,"page":240,"physical_pdf_page":253,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(فعلى\nالأول) أي القول بوجود القدرة مع الفعل لا قبله (الممنوع) الذي يمنع\nمن فعل يصح صدوره عنه في الجملة (لا يكون قادرا) إذ لا فعل حينئذ فلا قدرة عليه","english":"“On the first” position, namely that power exists together with the act and not before it, “the prevented person,” who is prevented from an act that can in general proceed from him, “is not capable”: there is then no act, and therefore no power over it—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I241.upper.01","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I241.lower.01","volume":1,"page":241,"physical_pdf_page":254,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"من الفعل ليس قادرا بالفعل إذ لا فعل حينئذ حتى يتعلق قدرته به وأما القدرة بمعنى مبدأ\nالتغيير فلم ينكروا وجودها في الممنوع غايته أنها لا تتعلق إلا عند الفعل فلا نزاع\nمعنى مع المعتزلة","english":"[The apparent, acceptable interpretation is that the person prevented] from acting is not capable in actuality, since at that point there is no act for his power to relate to. As for power in the sense of the principle of change, they did not deny its existence in the prevented person. At most, it relates [to its object] only when the act occurs. There is thus no substantive disagreement with the Muʿtazila.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I240.lower.03","editorial":"The bracketed opening is supplied openly from the preceding page, not printed anew on this leaf.","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I241.lower.02","volume":1,"page":241,"physical_pdf_page":254,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وإنما ينافي المقدور الخ) هذه عبارة المصنف في شرح المقاصد\nنقلا عن المعتزلة والمعنى أن المنع من الفعل لا ينافي وجود صفة القدرة في الممنوع\nوإنما ينافي مقدورية المقدور أي تعلق القدرة به وإلا فليس له معنى محصل فاتضح\nحدس عدم النزاع","english":"On “It is incompatible only with the object of power, etc.” This is the author’s wording in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, reporting the Muʿtazila. Its meaning is that prevention from acting does not exclude the existence of the attribute of power in the prevented person; it excludes only the object’s being an object of that power—that is, the power’s relation to it. Otherwise, the expression has no determinate meaning. The surmise that there is no disagreement is thereby clarified.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I241.lower.03","volume":1,"page":241,"physical_pdf_page":254,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله للفرق الظاهر الخ) واستدل عليه أيضا بأن المقيد الممنوع\nلم يلحقه تغير في ذاته وصفته فقدرته موجودة عند القيد إذ هي من صفات نفسه\nبخلاف العاجز وأجيب بمنع عدم التغير في الصفة","english":"On “Because of the evident difference, etc.” Another argument for it is that the fettered, prevented person has undergone no change in his essence or attribute. His power therefore exists while he is fettered, since it is one of the attributes of his self, unlike the incapable person. The reply disputes the assertion that there has been no change in the attribute.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I241.lower.04","volume":1,"page":241,"physical_pdf_page":254,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إلا جريان عادة الله الخ)\nهذا الجواب وجواب الاستدلال الذي مر آنفا يدلان بظاهرهما على أن النزاع بين\nالفريقين معنوي فتدبر","english":"On “Except that God’s customary practice, etc.” This reply and the reply to the argument just mentioned apparently indicate that the disagreement between the two groups is substantive. Reflect on this.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","editorial":"This qualification is retained rather than harmonised with the earlier suggestion that the disagreement is verbal.","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I241.lower.05","volume":1,"page":241,"physical_pdf_page":254,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مغاير لما نجده عند الخ) ضرورة أن الشرائط المخصصة\nلهذا غير المخصصة لذاك كما يصرح به قريبا","english":"On “Differs from what we find when, etc.” Necessarily, the conditions that specify this act differ from those that specify that act, as he will state explicitly shortly.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I241.lower.06","volume":1,"page":241,"physical_pdf_page":254,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والحق الخ) هذا توفيق بين الفريقين\nأخذه من كلام الإمام حاصله أن القدرة بمعنى نفس القوة التي هي مبدأ التغيير كائنة\nمع الفعل وقبله وبعده سواء كان بقاؤها بنفسها أو بتجدد الأمثال وأما بمعنى القوة\nالمأخوذة مع جميع شرائط تأثيرها أو تسببها العادي التي من جملتها تعلقها بالمقدور","english":"On “The truth, etc.” This reconciles the two groups, and he took it from the Imām’s discussion. In summary: power in the sense of the faculty itself, which is the principle of change, exists together with the act, before it and after it, whether it persists in itself or through the renewal of like instances. But power in the sense of the faculty taken together with all the conditions of its efficacy, or of its customary causation—including its relation to the object of power—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I242.lower.01","readings":[],"proof_notes":["resolved by enlarged original; retained تسببها","Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I241.paratext.01","volume":1,"page":241,"physical_pdf_page":254,"layer":"paratext","attribution":"printed gathering signature","arabic":"(١٦ — تقريب اول)","english":"[Gathering signature:] 16 — Taqrīb, first [volume].","reading_status":"second_visual_read","editorial":"Printer’s gathering signature outside the main frame; it is paratext, not a marginal author’s annotation.","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I241.upper.01","volume":1,"page":241,"physical_pdf_page":254,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(كالزمن) الذي هو عاجز عن الفعل وعلى القول الثاني الممنوع ليس\nكالزمن بل هو قادر والمنع لا ينافي القدرة وإنما ينافي المقدور وذلك للفرق الظاهر\nبين الصحيح الممنوع عن الفعل والزمن الذي أصابته آفة القدرة قلنا لا فرق إلا\nجريان عادة الله تعالى بخلق القدرة في الممنوع المقيد حال ارتفاع القيد لأن\nارتفاعه معتاد وعدمه في الزمن فإن ارتفاع زمانته غير معتاد","english":"“Like the chronically disabled person,” who is incapable of the act. On the second position, however, the person prevented from acting is not like the chronically disabled person: he is capable. Prevention is not incompatible with power; it is incompatible only with its object. This is because of the evident difference between a healthy person prevented from acting and a chronically disabled person whose power has been impaired. We reply: the only difference is that God’s customary practice is to create power in the prevented, fettered person when the fetter is removed, since its removal is customary, and not to create it in the chronically disabled person, since the removal of his chronic disability is not customary.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I240.upper.04","editorial":"The quoted comparison completes the upper sentence begun on I:240. The final negation concerns the customary creation of power, not an absolute denial of possible recovery.","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I241.upper.02","volume":1,"page":241,"physical_pdf_page":254,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وكذا) على الأول\n(القدرة الواحدة لا تتعلق بمقدورين) لأنها مع الفعل ولا شك أن ما نجد عند\nصدور أحد المقدورين مغاير لما نجده عند صدور الآخر","english":"“Likewise,” on the first position, “one and the same power does not relate to two objects of power,” because it accompanies the act. There is no doubt that what we find when one of the two possible acts proceeds differs from what we find when the other proceeds.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I241.upper.03","volume":1,"page":241,"physical_pdf_page":254,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والحق أن) القدرة بمعنى\n(القوة التي هي مبدأ الأفعال المختلفة) وهي التي حيثما انضمت إليها إرادة أحد","english":"“The truth is that” power in the sense of “the faculty that is the principle of different acts”—the faculty to which, whenever the will for one [of the two contraries] is conjoined—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I242.upper.01","proof_notes":["Stage68 closing comparison: full visible page compared with Arabic reading and English; incoming/outgoing joins retained. Same-assistant editorial proof, not independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I242.lower.01","volume":1,"page":242,"physical_pdf_page":255,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"بالفعل فهي مع الفعل ليس إلا","english":"[Including the power’s] actual [relation to the object of power]: it exists together with the act, and only then.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I241.lower.06","readings":[],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I242.lower.02","volume":1,"page":242,"physical_pdf_page":255,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وكأن ذلك مبني على منع الخ) يعني أن ما ذكر\nمن أن نفس القوة المذكورة توجد مع الفعل أو قبله وبعده إنما يتم على القول بجواز\nبقاء الأعراض وإلا فهي عرض لا يبقى زمانين على قاعدتهم هذا وأنت ما حررناه\nلك سابقا فهمت أن مدعاهم ليست مبنية على امتناع بقاء الأعراض بل بقائها\nبالتجدد يكفي فيما هم بصدده ولعل الأمر بالفهم للإشارة إلى ما ذكرنا فتدبر فإنه دقيق","english":"On “It would seem that this is based on rejecting, etc.” He means that what was said—that the faculty itself exists together with the act, or before it and after it—holds only on the view that accidents can persist. Otherwise it is an accident and, on their rule, cannot persist through two times. Yet, from what we established for you earlier, you will have understood that their claim is not based on the impossibility of accidents’ persistence: persistence through renewal suffices for their purpose. Perhaps his injunction to understand points to what we have said. Reflect on it, for it is subtle.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","readings":[{"locus":"هذا وأنت ما حررناه","reading":"هذا وأنت ما حررناه","status":"printed compressed syntax; no silent inserted Arabic"}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I242.lower.03","volume":1,"page":242,"physical_pdf_page":255,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فالزمن عاجز الخ) أي عن أن يدفع القعود من نفسه لا عن أن يأتي القيام\nالمعدوم","english":"On “The chronically disabled person is incapable, etc.” That is, incapable of removing sitting from himself, not incapable of bringing about the nonexistent standing.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I242.lower.04","volume":1,"page":242,"physical_pdf_page":255,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بعد تسليم كونه وجوديا الخ) فإن كونه وجوديا ممنوع لجواز أن يكون\nعدم ملكة كما يأتي","english":"On “Even if its being something positive is conceded, etc.” Its being something positive is disputed, since it may be a privation, as will follow.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I242.lower.05","volume":1,"page":242,"physical_pdf_page":255,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لا عن السكوت الخ) أي لا عن دفع السكوت فإنهم قادرون","english":"On “Not with respect to silence, etc.” That is, not with respect to removing silence, since they are capable—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I243.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I242.upper.01","volume":1,"page":242,"physical_pdf_page":255,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الضدين حصل ذلك الضد (تأثيرا) عند المعتزلة (أو تسببا عاديا) عند الأشاعرة\n(توجد مع الفعل وقبله وبعده) بمعنى القوة التي تكون (مع جميع شرائط\nالتأثير) أو التسبب العادي (لا تكون إلا معه) ويمتنع تعلقها بمقدورين فإن\nالشرائط لهذا غيرها لذاك هذا وكأن ذلك مبني على منع امتناع بقاء الأعراض\nفافهم","english":"[The faculty is such that, whenever the will for one] of the two contraries is conjoined to it, that contrary occurs—“through efficacy,” according to the Muʿtazila, “or through customary causation,” according to the Ashʿarīs. [Power in this sense] “exists together with the act, before it and after it.” In the sense of the faculty taken “together with all the conditions of efficacy,” or of customary causation, [power] “exists only together with it.” It cannot relate to two objects of power, since the conditions for this one differ from those for that one. It would seem that this is based on rejecting the impossibility of accidents’ persistence. Understand this.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I241.upper.03","editorial":"The printed passage moves from the unqualified faculty to the faculty together with all specifying conditions. Square brackets clarify this shift without silently adding it to the Arabic.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I242.upper.02","volume":1,"page":242,"physical_pdf_page":255,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والعجز) المقابل للقدرة (قيل هو) وجودي (ضد القدرة) للقطع\nبأن في الزمن معنى لا يوجد في الممنوع من الفعل مع اشتراكهما في عدم\nالتمكن من الفعل فإن كل عاقل يجد من نفسه تفرقة بين كونه زمنا وكونه\nممنوعا من القيام مع سلامته وما هي إلا أن في الزمن صفة وجودية هي العجز ليست\nفي الممنوع (فلا تعلق إلا بالموجود) لأن تعلق الموجود بالمعدوم خيال محض\nفالزمن عاجز عن القعود الموجود لا عن القيام المعدوم ورد بأنه بعد تسليم كونه\nوجوديا مكابرة فلا امتناع في تعلقه بالمعدوم كالعلم والإرادة ولهذا أطبق العقلاء\nعلى أن عجز المتحدى إنما هو عن الإتيان بالمثل المعدوم","english":"“Incapacity,” which is opposed to power: “it has been said to be” something positive, “the contrary of power.” The argument is that we are certain of something in the chronically disabled person that is not found in the person prevented from acting, although both lack the ability to act. Every rational person finds in himself a distinction between being chronically disabled and, while sound, being prevented from standing. This amounts to there being in the disabled person a positive attribute—incapacity—that is absent from the prevented person. “It therefore relates only to what exists,” since the relation of an existent to a nonexistent is [alleged to be] mere fancy. The disabled person is thus incapable with respect to the existent sitting, not with respect to the nonexistent standing. The reply is that, even if its being something positive is conceded, this is obstinate denial: there is nothing impossible in its relating to a nonexistent, just as knowledge and will do. For this reason, rational people unanimously maintain that the incapacity of a person challenged to produce a counterpart concerns the nonexistent production of that counterpart.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","editorial":"The transmitted phrase عجز المتحدى من إتيانه is awkward. The translation retains its argumentative referent rather than replacing the Arabic. Positive/existent describes the proposed ontological status of incapacity, not its moral value.","proof_notes":["Removed invented من إتيانه and restored visible إنما; compared complete upper paragraph with native source image."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I242.upper.03","volume":1,"page":242,"physical_pdf_page":255,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وقيل) هو (عدم ملكة)\nالقدرة (للقطع بأن عجز المتحدى) بمعارضة القرآن (إنما هو عن الإتيان بالمثل)\nالمعدوم لا عن السكوت وعدم الإتيان بالمثل لأن العقل يحكم بأن المعارضة إنما هي","english":"“It has also been said” that incapacity “is a privation” of power, “because it is certain that the incapacity of the person challenged”—to rival the Qurʾān—“concerns only the production of a counterpart,” which does not exist, not silence and the failure to produce a counterpart. Reason judges that the challenge concerns only—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I243.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I243.lower.01","volume":1,"page":243,"physical_pdf_page":256,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"على مطلق النطق وإنما عجزهم عن النطق المخصوص الذي هو مثل القرآن وهو معدوم\nبل ممتنع فالسكوت عن المثل وترك الإتيان به موجود ومقدور لهم","english":"[They are capable] of speech in general. Their incapacity concerns only the specific speech that would be a counterpart of the Qurʾān; that is nonexistent, indeed impossible. Silence instead of such a counterpart, and refraining from producing it, exist and are within their power.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I242.lower.05","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I243.lower.02","volume":1,"page":243,"physical_pdf_page":256,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وحديث\nالتفرقة الخ) يعني سلمنا أن بين المعارض والممنوع تفرقة لكنها ليست تفرقة موجبة\nلكون العجز ضد القدرة بل هي تفرقة بعكس ما ذكرتم موجبة لكونه عدم ملكة\nالقدرة","english":"On “The alleged difference, etc.” This means: we concede a difference between the person attempting a rival production and the prevented person, but it is not a difference that entails incapacity’s being the contrary of power. Rather, reversing what you said, it is a difference that entails its being a privation of power.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","readings":[{"locus":"المعارض","reading":"المعارض","status":"source wording retained; main discussion concerns الزمن"}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I243.lower.03","volume":1,"page":243,"physical_pdf_page":256,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بين المعنيين الخ) أي بين الصفة التي تستعقب الفعل لا عن قدرة فيكون\nوجوديا وبين عدم القدرة فيكون عدميا","english":"On “Between the two meanings, etc.” That is, between an attribute followed by an act not arising from power—in which case incapacity would be positive—and the absence of power—in which case it would be privative.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I243.lower.04","volume":1,"page":243,"physical_pdf_page":256,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لم يجئ نقله مشتركا الخ) أي لا بحسب اللغة ولا\nبحسب العرف كما في شرح المقاصد إن قيل نقل الاختلاف في معناه يقتضي اشتراكه لأن\nكلا من المخالفين يدعي إطلاقه على ما ادعاه (قلت) إنما يتم لو لم ينكر كل منهما\nإطلاقه على المعنى الذي يقول به الآخر وليس كذلك فإن الحاصل هو أنهما متفقان\nعلى أن لفظ العجز ليس مشتركا بل له معنى واحد لكنهما مختلفان في أن ذلك المعنى\nالواحد هو وجودي أو عدمي فيكون قوله وجعله مشتركا الخ جوابا عما يقال لم لا يجوز\nأن يكون الخلف بينهما راجعا إلى اللفظ فتأمل وإن سلم أن القول بأنه عدم ملكة","english":"On “No transmission establishes it as equivocal, etc.” That is, neither in language nor in customary usage, as in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid. If it is said: reporting a disagreement over its meaning entails equivocity, because each disputant claims that the word is applied to the meaning he proposes, I reply: this would follow only if neither denied its application to the meaning proposed by the other. That is not the case. Rather, they agree that the word “incapacity” is not equivocal but has one meaning; they disagree over whether that one meaning is positive or privative. Thus, “Making it equivocal, etc.” answers the question: might their disagreement concern the wording? Reflect. Even if it is conceded that the view that it is a privation—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I244.lower.01","editorial":"The source reads لم لا يجوز: “Why may it not be …?” This is an explicit question, not a conjecturally supplied interrogative.","proof_notes":["Third visual comparison: restored the visible لم before لا يجوز; interrogative sense confirmed from enlarged native image."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I243.upper.01","volume":1,"page":243,"physical_pdf_page":256,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"بالأمثال لا بأعدامها وحديث التفرقة بين الزمن والممنوع معكوس بأن في الممنوع\nصفة وجودية هي القدرة دون الزمن (وجعله) أي لفظ العجز (مشتركا بين\nالمعنيين) حتى يكون الزمن عاجزا عن القعود بالمعنى الأول دون الثاني وعن القيام\nبالمعنى الثاني دون الأول (خلاف اللغة) لم يجئ نقله مشتركا فالكلام في المتعارف\nالشائع الاستعمال","english":"[The challenge concerns producing] counterparts, not their absences. The alleged difference between the chronically disabled and the prevented person can be reversed: the prevented person has a positive attribute—power—which the disabled person lacks. “Making it”—the word “incapacity”—“equivocal between the two meanings,” so that the disabled person is incapable with respect to sitting in the first sense but not the second, and with respect to standing in the second but not the first, “is contrary to linguistic usage.” No transmission establishes it as equivocal; the discussion concerns what is familiar and commonly used.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I242.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I243.upper.02","volume":1,"page":243,"physical_pdf_page":256,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والقدرة تضاد الخلق) وهو ملكة يصدر عن النفس\nبسببها أفعال بسهولة من غير تقدم فكر وروية فغير الراسخ من صفات النفس لا يكون\nخلقا كغضب الحليم وكذا الراسخ الذي هو مبدأ أفعال الجوارح بسهولة ولما\nكانت القدرة يصدر منها الفعل لا بسهولة واستغناء عن روية وكانت نسبتها إلى الفعل\nوالترك على السواء حكم بأنها تضاد الخلق (لما أن أفعاله) كالكرم صادرة بسهولة\n(بلا) تقدم تفكر و(روية) ولا يكون صالحا لأن يقع به الطرفان فمعنى تضادهما","english":"“Power is contrary to character”: character is a disposition through which acts proceed from the soul easily, without prior thought and deliberation. A non-established attribute of the soul, such as the anger of a forbearing person, is therefore not character; neither is an established disposition that is the principle of easy acts of the bodily members. Since acts proceed from power without [necessarily] proceeding easily or dispensing with deliberation, and since its relation to acting and refraining is equal, it was judged contrary to character, “because its acts,” such as generosity, proceed easily, “without” prior thought and “deliberation”; it is not apt for both alternatives to occur through it. Thus the meaning of their contrariety—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I244.upper.01","readings":[],"editorial":"The established disposition of bodily skill is excluded from خلق in this restricted definition. The end of the paragraph denies that character is equally apt for both alternatives; it does not posit two “contrary states.”","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I244.lower.01","volume":1,"page":244,"physical_pdf_page":257,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"لا يلزمه القول بأن هناك أمرا وجوديا لكن القول بأنه وجودي يلزمه القول بأن ذلك\nالوجودي ليس بقدرة فإن كلا من الضادين يصدق عليه أنه أمر وجودي يصدق\nعليه أنه ليس هو الضاد الآخر فلا يطلق لفظ العجز عند القائل بأنه وجودي تارة على\nذلك الوجودي وتارة على لازمه أعني عدم القدرة كما يقال ذلك في لفظ المرض على ما يأتي\nوحاصل الجواب أن الإطلاق على المعنيين لم ينقل حتى يجعل النزاع لفظيا فتدبر\nجدا","english":"[Even if it is conceded that saying incapacity is a privation] does not entail affirming a positive entity, whereas saying it is positive entails saying that this positive entity is not power—for each of two contraries can be described as a positive entity and as not being the other contrary—[it does not follow that] the proponent of positive incapacity applies the word “incapacity” sometimes to that positive entity and sometimes to its concomitant, namely the absence of power, as is said concerning the word “disease,” as will follow. In summary, application to the two meanings has not been transmitted, so that the disagreement could be made verbal. Reflect very carefully.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I243.lower.04","editorial":"The concessive structure crosses the page boundary. The English bracket records the suppressed inference rather than creating a new Arabic sentence.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I244.lower.02","volume":1,"page":244,"physical_pdf_page":257,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله تضاد أحكامهما الخ) يعني يمتنع اجتماعهما على فعل من جهة واحدة لأن\nالشيء قد يكون ملائما الخ فإدراكه من جهة الملاءمة لا يكون لذة كالصفراوي لا يلتذ\nبالحلو وبقيد الحيثية يندفع ما يقال إن المريض قد يلتذ بالحلاوة مع أنها لا تلائمه بل\nتضره وينفر من الأدوية مع أنها تلائمه وتنفعه","english":"On “The opposition of their consequences, etc.” He means that they cannot coincide concerning one act in the same respect. [The gloss then turns to congeniality:] “For a thing may be congenial, etc.” Its apprehension from the aspect of congeniality is not pleasure—as the bilious person takes no pleasure in sweetness. [This sentence is translated as printed; its formulation is problematic.] By the qualification “insofar as,” the following objection is answered: a sick person may take pleasure in sweetness although it is not congenial to him but harms him, and may recoil from medicines although they are congenial to him and benefit him.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","readings":[{"locus":"فإدراكه من جهة الملاءمة لا يكون لذة","reading":"The visible wording is retained. The surrounding argument apparently needs a distinction between apprehending the thing and apprehending it precisely as congenial; no absent غير or other repair has been silently supplied.","status":"semantic crux; external parallel or second witness required"}],"editorial":"Corrected an earlier reversal: وبقيد الحيثية يندفع ما يقال means that the qualification answers the objection. It does not mean that the qualification is defeated by the objection. The preceding sentence remains a separate source crux.","proof_notes":["Compared with the original 2547-pixel scan, not only its reduced preview: وبقيد الحيثية يندفع confirmed; preceding congeniality clause held unresolved."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I244.lower.03","volume":1,"page":244,"physical_pdf_page":257,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والوصول إلى ذات المدرك الخ)\nوالظاهر من كلامهم هو أن اللذة لا تتم بحصول الصورة المساوية الذهنية للذيذ فقط","english":"On “And reaching the apprehended thing itself, etc.” What their words apparently mean is that pleasure is not completed merely by obtaining the corresponding mental form of the pleasurable thing—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I245.lower.context","editorial":"The continuation on I:245 explicitly also denies that the bare presence of the thing alone suffices; both apprehension and attainment are required.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I244.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":244,"physical_pdf_page":257,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لا إلى مجرد صورته الخ) كما في الإدراك المذكور سابقا المعدود في أقسام الكيفيات\nاه منه","english":"On “Not merely its form, etc.” As in the apprehension mentioned earlier and counted among the divisions of qualities. End. From him.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","editorial":"The printed signature منه is preserved as a source attribution marker; its personal author is not identified here.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I244.upper.01","volume":1,"page":244,"physical_pdf_page":257,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"تضاد أحكامهما (وهل) هي (تضاد النوم فيه تردد) لاختلاف في جواز بعض\nالأفعال من النائم وامتناع البعض واختلفوا فيما صدر عنه فذهب المعتزلة وبعض\nأصحابنا إلى أنه مقدور له والنوم لا يضاد القدرة وقال الأستاذ أبو إسحق بالتضاد\nوتوقف القاضي أبو بكر وكثير من أصحابنا","english":"[Their contrariety means] the opposition of their consequences. “Whether” power “is contrary to sleep is uncertain,” because of disagreement concerning the possibility of some acts on the sleeper’s part and the impossibility of others. They also disagreed about the acts that proceed from him. The Muʿtazila and some of our companions held that such an act is within his power, and that sleep is not contrary to power. The Master Abū Isḥāq affirmed their contrariety; the Qāḍī Abū Bakr and many of our companions suspended judgment.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_in":"I243.upper.02","readings":[],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I244.upper.02","volume":1,"page":244,"physical_pdf_page":257,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ومنها) أي من الكيفيات النفسانية\n(اللذة والألم) وتصورهما بديهي كسائر الوجدانيات (وقد فهم من تفسيرهما)\nقصدا إلى تعيين المسمى (بإدراك الملائم والمنافر من حيث هما كذلك) أي ملائم\nومنافر لأن الشيء قد يكون ملائما من وجه دون وجه واللذة إدراكه من حيث إنه\nملائم والألم إدراكه من حيث إنه منافِر (أنهما نوعان من الإدراك) اعتبر فيهما الإضافة\nإلى الملائم والمنافر (على احتمال أن يراد) من الإدراك (الإصابة والوجدان)\nوالوصول إلى ذات المدرك لا إلى مجرد صورته لأن تخيل اللذيذ غير اللذة واللذة لا تتم\nبحصول ما يساوي اللذيذ بل بحصول ذاته","english":"“Among them”—that is, among the psychological qualities—are “pleasure and pain.” Their concepts are self-evident, like those of other immediately experienced states. “It has been understood from their explanation”—intended to identify what the terms designate—“as apprehending what is congenial and what is uncongenial precisely as such,” that is, as congenial or uncongenial, since a thing may be congenial in one respect but not another, and pleasure is apprehending it insofar as it is congenial, while pain is apprehending it insofar as it is uncongenial—“that they are two species of apprehension,” considered in relation to the congenial and uncongenial. This allows “the possibility that what is intended” by apprehension “is actual encounter and finding”: reaching the apprehended thing itself, not merely its form. For imagining the pleasurable thing is not pleasure, and pleasure is not completed by obtaining something equivalent to the pleasurable thing, but by obtaining the thing itself.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","readings":[],"editorial":"The original explicitly contrasts تخيل اللذيذ with اللذة. The separate ruled note and the lower gloss qualify this account; they are translated independently below.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I244.upper.03","volume":1,"page":244,"physical_pdf_page":257,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وبعضهم على أن اللذة خروج عن","english":"“Some hold that pleasure is departure from”—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","join_out":"I245.upper.context","editorial":"Continues on I:245: الحالة الغير الطبيعية, “the non-natural condition.”","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I245.lower.01","volume":1,"page":245,"physical_pdf_page":258,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ولا بحصول ذاته فقط بل إنما تتم بكليهما يعني الإدراك الوصولي النيلي وهو المراد بالإصابة والوجدان فقوله بحصول ذاته أي بوصوله إدراكا","english":"—nor merely through obtaining the thing itself. Rather, pleasure is completed only by both: namely, apprehension that reaches and attains its object. This is what is intended by “encountering” and “finding.” Thus “through obtaining the thing itself” means attaining it in apprehension.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I245.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I244.lower.03","proof_notes":["Lower enlargement reads النيلي, from نيل, not الفعلي. Preserves the terminological pairing of وصول and نيل.","Complete upper and lower source regions reread; I244 incoming gloss and I246 outgoing gloss kept distinct and linked. No second witness or independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I245.lower.02","volume":1,"page":245,"physical_pdf_page":258,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولا شك أن الكمال الخ) قد سبق الفرق بين الكمال والخير وأنه يدركه الخ أي يحصل له ذلك المعقول بحقيقته لأن العقل يصل إلى كنه الخ فما مر من الوصول إلى ذات اللذيذ هو بمعنى الوصول إلى حقيقته المحققة في نفس الأمر أعم من أن يكون إلى كنهها أو ظاهرها وهذا بيان كونه أقوى كيفية وأما بيان كونه أقوى كمية فهو أن عدد تفاصيل المعقولات أكثر بل يكاد أن لا يحصى","english":"On “There is no doubt that the perfection, etc.” The distinction between perfection and good has already been given. “And it apprehends it, etc.”: that is, the intelligible is acquired in its reality, since the intellect reaches its inner nature. The previous expression “reaching the pleasurable thing itself” therefore means reaching its reality as established in the order of things, whether its inner nature or its outward aspect. This explains why intellectual pleasure is stronger qualitatively. Its greater quantitative extent is explained by the fact that the detailed intelligibles are more numerous—indeed, almost innumerable.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I245.jpg","stage":69,"proof_notes":["Complete upper and lower source regions reread; I244 incoming gloss and I246 outgoing gloss kept distinct and linked. No second witness or independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I245.lower.03","volume":1,"page":245,"physical_pdf_page":258,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والظاهر أنهما ليسا من جنس الخ) قد سبق أنهما لا يتمان بواحد من الإدراك والنيل بل لا بد لتحققهما من كليهما وما","english":"On “Yet it appears that they do not belong to the genus, etc.” It was previously stated that neither is completed by either apprehension or attainment alone: both are required for their realisation. As for—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I245.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I246.lower.01","proof_notes":["Complete upper and lower source regions reread; I244 incoming gloss and I246 outgoing gloss kept distinct and linked. No second witness or independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I245.upper.01","volume":1,"page":245,"physical_pdf_page":258,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الحالة الغير الطبيعية) وهو معنى الخلاص عن الألم كالأكل فإنه دفع لألم الجوع وأبطله ابن سينا بأنه قد يحصل اللذة من غير سابقة ألم وحالة غير طبيعية كما في مصادفة مال ومطالعة جمال من غير طلب","english":"—“the unnatural condition.” This is what release from pain means, as with eating, for it removes the pain of hunger. Ibn Sīnā refuted this by observing that pleasure can occur without preceding pain or an unnatural condition: for example, unexpectedly finding wealth or beholding beauty without having sought it.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I245.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I244.upper.final","proof_notes":["Upper enlargement confirms كالأكل, “as with eating”; repeated كلا was a typing/transcription error, not a source reading.","Complete upper and lower source regions reread; I244 incoming gloss and I246 outgoing gloss kept distinct and linked. No second witness or independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I245.upper.02","volume":1,"page":245,"physical_pdf_page":258,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وكل) من اللذة والألم (إما حسي) كتكيف العضو الذائق بالحلاوة والمرارة (أو عقلي) كأن يتمثل في العقل ما يعقل تمثلا مطابقا خاليا عن شوائب الظنون والأوهام ولا شك أن هذا الكمال خير للجوهر العاقل وأنه يدركه ويدرك حصوله له فإذن تلذذ بإدراك هذا الكمال وهذا هو اللذة العقلية والألم العقلي هو أن يحصل له ضد هذا الكمال ويدرك حصوله من حيث هو ضد (وهو) أي العقلي منهما (أقوى) لأن العقل يصل إلى كنه المعقول والحس لا يدرك إلا ما يتعلق بظواهر الأجسام فيكون الإدراك العقلي أقوى وأتم فكذا اللذة والألم التابعان له","english":"“Each” of pleasure and pain “is either sensory”—as when the organ of taste is affected by sweetness or bitterness—“or intellectual”: for example, when an intelligible is represented in the intellect accurately, free of the admixture of conjectures and imaginings. This perfection is undoubtedly good for an intellectual substance, which apprehends both the perfection and its acquisition. It therefore takes pleasure in apprehending this perfection: this is intellectual pleasure. Intellectual pain consists in the acquisition of the contrary of this perfection and apprehension of that acquisition precisely as contrary. “It”—that is, the intellectual instance of either—“is stronger.” The intellect reaches the inner reality of the intelligible, whereas sense apprehends only what concerns the outward appearances of bodies. Intellectual apprehension is consequently stronger and more complete; the pleasure and pain consequent upon it are likewise so.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I245.jpg","stage":69,"proof_notes":["Enlargement resolves فإذن, “therefore,” not conditional فإذا. Existing English already expressed the inference.","Complete upper and lower source regions reread; I244 incoming gloss and I246 outgoing gloss kept distinct and linked. No second witness or independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I245.upper.03","volume":1,"page":245,"physical_pdf_page":258,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فإن قيل الحسي من اللذة والألم ينبغي أن يعد من الكيفيات المحسوسة دون النفسانية قلنا المدرك بالحس هو الكيفية التي يتلذذ بها ويتألم كالحلاوة والمرارة مثلا وأما نفس اللذة والألم التي هي جنس الإدراك والنيل فلا سبيل للحواس الظاهرة إلى إدراكها هذا والظاهر أنهما ليسا من جنس الإدراك والنيل وإن كانا مشروطين بهما لكنهما كيفيتان","english":"If it is objected that sensory pleasure and pain should be included among sensible rather than psychic qualities, we reply: what sense apprehends is the quality that gives pleasure or pain—for example, sweetness or bitterness. As for pleasure and pain themselves, which belong to the genus of apprehension and attainment, the external senses have no access to them. That is the account. Yet it appears that they do not belong to the genus of apprehension and attainment, although both are conditions for them. Rather, they are two qualities—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I245.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I246.upper.01","editorial":"The apparent authorial qualification beginning هذا والظاهر is retained; it must not be harmonised with the preceding definition.","proof_notes":["Complete upper and lower source regions reread; I244 incoming gloss and I246 outgoing gloss kept distinct and linked. No second witness or independent human review."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I246.lower.01","volume":1,"page":246,"physical_pdf_page":259,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أنهما هل هما نفس الإدراك الوصولي الذي هو بطريق النيل أو مترتبان عليه فيه تردد فتدبر","english":"—whether the two are themselves the apprehension that reaches its object by attainment, or are consequences of it, remains uncertain. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I246.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I245.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I246.lower.02","volume":1,"page":246,"physical_pdf_page":259,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إلا أنه قدمها الخ) أعني الذكر مع أنها متأخرة في الوجود حيث يكون أولا حالا ثم ملكة (قوله للاتفاق الخ) ولأنها لرسوخها أشرف من الحال ثم التعريف إنما يتناول صحة الحيوان دون النبات لأن الملكة والحال من الكيفيات النفسانية الحيوانية فإن قيل إنه يتناول صحة النبات أيضا فبني على أن يراد بهما الراسخ وغير الراسخ من مطلق الكيفية أو النفس أعم من الحيوانية والنباتية","english":"On “Except that he mentioned it first, etc.”: first in the order of mention, although it is later in existence, since it is initially a transient condition and subsequently a settled disposition. On “Because of agreement, etc.”: also because its stability makes it nobler than a transient condition. The definition covers animal health, not plant health, because settled dispositions and transient conditions belong to animal psychic qualities. To say that it includes plant health too presupposes that these terms mean stable and unstable qualities without restriction, or that “soul” includes both animal and vegetative soul.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I246.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I246.lower.03","volume":1,"page":246,"physical_pdf_page":259,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والسلامة في الأفعال الخ) جواب عما قيل إن تعريف الصحة بما ذكر دوري وذلك لذكر قيد السلامة المرادفة للصحة فيه وحاصله أن المعرف صحة البدن والعضو","english":"On “Soundness in actions, etc.” This answers the objection that the definition of health is circular because it includes soundness, a synonym of health. The answer is that what is being defined is health of the body and organ—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I246.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I247.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I246.upper.01","volume":1,"page":246,"physical_pdf_page":259,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"عارضتان للنفس ولو في الحسي (والحسي من الألم عند اللمس يسمى وجعا) فإن لفظ الوجع مختص بالحس في العرف بل قال المصنف الظاهر اختصاصه باللمس على ما صرح به العضد وإن كان ظاهره كلام أئمة اللغة أنه مرادف الألم","english":"—that occur in the soul, even in the sensory case. “Sensory pain involving touch is called aching [wajaʿ].” In customary usage the word wajaʿ is restricted to sensory pain. Indeed, the author says: “It appears to be restricted to touch,” as al-ʿAḍud explicitly states, although the apparent wording of the leading lexicographers makes it synonymous with pain.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I246.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I245.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I246.upper.02","volume":1,"page":246,"physical_pdf_page":259,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ومنها الصحة والمرض فالصحة ملكة أو حالة) ليست أو كأنها للتردد المنافي للتحديد بل للتنبيه على أن جنس الصحة هو الكيفية النفسانية سواء كانت راسخة أو غير راسخة ولا تختص بالراسخة كما زعم البعض الأئمة فقدموها للاتفاق على كونها صحة دون غير الراسخة فقيل إنها صحة أيضا وقيل واسطة وقوله (يصدر عنها الأفعال) يشعر بأن المبدأ هو تلك الكيفية وقوله (من الموضوع لها) يشعر بأنه الموضوع المراد به البدن أو العضو والجواب عنه بوجهين أحدهما أن الصحة مبدأ فاعل والموضوع قابل والمعنى يصدر عنها الأفعال الكائنة من الموضوع الحاصل فيه وثانيهما أن الموضوع فاعل والصحة واسطة والمعنى يصدر لأجلها وبواسطتها","english":"“Among them are health and disease. Health is a settled disposition or a transient condition.” The “or” is not an expression of indecision incompatible with definition. It draws attention to the fact that the genus of health is psychic quality, whether firmly established or not; health is not restricted to the established kind, as some leading scholars maintain. The established disposition was mentioned first because its being health is agreed upon. The transient condition, by contrast, is said by some to be health and by others to be intermediate. The phrase “from which actions issue” suggests that the originating principle is that quality. The phrase “from its subject” suggests that it is the subject, meaning the body or an organ. This is answered in two ways. First, health is an active principle and the subject is receptive: the meaning is that the actions occurring from the subject in which health inheres issue from health. Second, the subject is the agent and health is an intermediary: the meaning is that actions issue on account of health and through its mediation.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I246.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I246.upper.03","volume":1,"page":246,"physical_pdf_page":259,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وقوله (سليمة) سالمة من الآفات والسلامة في الأفعال محسوسة وفي البدن غير محسوسة فتعرف الثانية بالأولى لكونها أجلى فلا دور (والمرض ملكة أو حالة مضادة لها) أي الصحة فهو كيفية يصدر عنها الأفعال غير سليمة فيكون وجوديا (وقيل عدمها عما يمكنها) فحينئذ لا يكون من الكيفيات وقد يوفق بين القولين","english":"“Sound” means free of defects. Soundness in actions is perceptible, whereas soundness in the body is not; the latter is therefore known through the former, which is more evident, so there is no circularity. “Disease is a settled disposition or transient condition contrary to it,” that is, to health. It is thus a quality from which actions issue unsoundly, and so is something positive. “It is also said to be its absence from what is capable of it.” In that case, disease is not a quality. The two accounts may be reconciled—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I246.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I247.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I247.lower.01","volume":1,"page":247,"physical_pdf_page":260,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وهي غير محسوسة والمأخوذ في التعريف صحة الأفعال وهي محسوسة فلا دور","english":"—which is not perceptible; what enters the definition is the health of actions, which is perceptible. Hence there is no circularity.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I247.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I246.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I247.lower.02","volume":1,"page":247,"physical_pdf_page":260,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وكأنه مبني الخ) وكأنه مبني على نقل استعمال لفظ المرض في المعنيين بحسب اللغة والعرف (قوله ليست صحة ولا مرضا الخ) ليس الخلاف في ثبوت حالة لا يصدق عليها الصحة ولا المرض فإنها كثيرة كالعلم والجهل والقدرة ونحوها بل الخلاف في ثبوت حالة لا يصدق معها على البدن أنه صحيح أو مريض بل يصدق عليه أنه ليس بصحيح ولا مريض","english":"On “This seems to presuppose, etc.” It seems to presuppose transmitted evidence that “disease” is used in both senses in language and customary usage. On “Neither health nor disease, etc.” The dispute is not about the existence of a condition of which neither health nor disease is predicable: there are many such conditions, including knowledge, ignorance, power and the like. Rather, it concerns a condition in which the body cannot be called either healthy or diseased, but can be said to be neither healthy nor diseased.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I247.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I247.lower.03","volume":1,"page":247,"physical_pdf_page":260,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وإلا يعتبر فيهما ذلك الخ) أقول فعلى عدم اعتبار سلامة الجميع وآفة الجميع تكون الكيفية التي هي حالة تصدر منها الأفعال الخ واسطة عند من اعتبر الرسوخ في الصحة والمرض كما سبق فلا يتم قوله فلا واسطة لكن المقصود واضح فافهم","english":"On “Otherwise, if this is not required in them, etc.” I say: even if soundness in every action and impairment in every action are not required, the quality that is a transient condition from which actions issue, etc., would still be intermediate according to those who require stability in health and disease, as above. The assertion “there is no intermediate state” therefore does not hold without qualification. The intention is nevertheless clear. Understand.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I247.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I247.lower.04","volume":1,"page":247,"physical_pdf_page":260,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وكأنه مبني عد ذلك المجموع الخ) إشارة إلى جواب ما استشكل به عد","english":"On “Counting that combination seems to depend, etc.” This points to an answer to the difficulty raised against counting—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I247.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I248.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I247.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":247,"physical_pdf_page":260,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وكالخلقة) أعاد الكاف لمغايرتها لما قبلها في الانفراد والتركيب اه منه","english":"On “And such as configuration”: he repeats “such as” because configuration differs from what preceded in being composite rather than simple. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I247.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I247.upper.01","volume":1,"page":247,"physical_pdf_page":260,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"بأنه عند المرض تزول كيفية الصحة وتحدث كيفية أخرى فإن جعل المرض عبارة عن عدم الأولى فبينهما تقابل العدم والملكة وإن جعل عبارة عن نفس الكيفية الثانية فتقابل التضاد وكأنه مبني على أن لفظ المرض مشترك بينهما أو حقيقة في أحدهما مجاز في الآخر (وقد يتسامح) فيهما (بجعلهما من المحسوسات) لكون الأفعال الصادرة عنهما من المحسوسات","english":"—by saying that in disease the quality of health disappears and another quality arises. If disease means the absence of the first, the opposition is that of privation and possession. If it means the second quality itself, the opposition is contrariety. This seems to presuppose that “disease” is equivocal between the two, or literal in one meaning and figurative in the other. “The usage is sometimes relaxed by making both sensible qualities,” since the actions issuing from them are perceptible.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I247.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I246.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I247.upper.02","volume":1,"page":247,"physical_pdf_page":260,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ثم إذا اعتبر فيهما سلامة جميع الأفعال وآفة الجميع كانت بينهما واسطة) ليست صحة ولا مرضا (كالأطفال والمشائخ وإلا) يعتبر فيهما ذلك (فلا) واسطة هذا (الثالث) من أقسام الكيف (الكيفيات المختصة بالكميات) وهي التي ليس عروضها بالذات إلا للكم متصلا كان (كالاستقامة والانحناء) العارضين (للخطوط) و(كالتقعير والتقبيب) العارضين (للسطح) أو منفصلا (كالزوجية والفردية) العارضتين (للعدد)","english":"“If health requires soundness in all actions, and disease requires impairment in all, there is an intermediate state” that is neither health nor disease, “as in children and the aged. Otherwise”—if those conditions are not required—“there is no intermediate state.” That concludes this discussion. “The third” division of quality comprises “qualities proper to quantities”: qualities that belong essentially only to quantity, whether continuous—“such as straightness and curvature,” which belong “to lines,” and “concavity and convexity,” which belong “to surfaces”—or discrete, “such as evenness and oddness,” which belong “to number.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I247.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I247.upper.03","volume":1,"page":247,"physical_pdf_page":260,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ثم إنها تكون مفردة كما مر (و) مركبة (كالخلقة أعني مجموع الشكل واللون الذي يحسبه) أي بحسب المجموع (يوصف الشيء بالحسن والقبح) وكأنه مبني عد ذلك المجموع من الكيفيات المختصة بالكميات على ما قيل من","english":"These qualities may be simple, as in the foregoing examples, “or” composite, “such as configuration [khilqa], by which I mean the combination of shape and colour, on account of which”—that is, on account of the combination—“a thing is described as beautiful or ugly.” Counting this combination among qualities proper to quantities seems to depend on the assertion that—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I247.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I248.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"الذي يحسبه","status":"Read as الذي بحسبه (“on account of which”); the printed words require a character-level proof."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I248.lower.01","volume":1,"page":248,"physical_pdf_page":261,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"هذا المجموع من الكيفيات المختصة بالكميات من أن أحد جزأيه أعني الشكل وإن كان منها بناء على كون الشكل عبارة عن هيئة إحاطة حد أو حدود بالجسم كما في الكرة المحيط بها سطح واحد والمثلث والمربع الحاصلة من إحاطة خطوط والزاوية الحاصلة من إحاطة خطين لكن لا خفاء في أن جزأه الآخر أعني اللون من الكيفيات المحسوسة التي جعلناها مقابلة للكيفيات المختصة بالكميات وحاصل الجواب ظاهر وقد يقال لا نسلم أن اللون من خواص السطح فإنه قد ينفذ في عمق الجسم فتدبر","english":"—this combination among qualities proper to quantities. Although one of its two components, namely shape, belongs to that class—on the account that shape is the configuration of a boundary or boundaries enclosing a body, as in a sphere enclosed by one surface, triangles and quadrilaterals formed by enclosing lines, and an angle formed by two enclosing lines—its other component, colour, plainly belongs to sensible qualities, which we distinguished from qualities proper to quantities. The substance of the answer is evident. It may nevertheless be said: we do not concede that colour is a property of surface, for it may penetrate the depth of a body. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I248.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I247.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I248.lower.02","volume":1,"page":248,"physical_pdf_page":261,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لكان هناك أقسام الخ) مثل اللون أو الضوء مع الاستقامة أو الانحناء أو الزوجية أو الفردية إلى غير ذلك (قوله أو كيفية حاصلة الخ) أو الشكل المنضم إلى اللون كما في شرح المقاصد","english":"On “There would be divisions, etc.” For example, colour or light together with straightness, curvature, evenness, oddness, and so forth. On “Or a resulting quality, etc.” Alternatively, shape conjoined with colour, as stated in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I248.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I248.lower.03","volume":1,"page":248,"physical_pdf_page":261,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهذا أقرب إلى جعلها الخ) لأنه إذا كانت عبارة عن مجموع الشكل واللون تكون وحدته من حيث التركيب الانضمامي من شيئين مختلفين بالنوع بخلاف ما إذا كانت عبارة عن الكيفية الحاصلة منهما فإنها واحدة بالحقيقة وإن كانت حاصلة من مختلفين وحينئذ عدها من المركبات باعتبار حصولها من المركب لا لأنها مركبة في نفسها فتدبر","english":"On “The latter is closer to making it, etc.” If configuration is the combination of shape and colour, its unity is that of an aggregate composed of two things different in species. If it is the quality resulting from them, by contrast, it is truly one, although produced from different things. It is then counted among composites because it arises from a composite, not because it is itself composite. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I248.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I248.lower.04","volume":1,"page":248,"physical_pdf_page":261,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وكالزاوية الخ) كتب مد ظله على قوله وكالخلقة أعاد الكاف لمغايرتها لما قبلها في الانفراد والتركيب","english":"On “Another example is the angle, etc.” [The writer]—may his protection endure—wrote on “And such as configuration”: “He repeats ‘such as’ because it differs from what preceded in being composite rather than simple—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I248.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I249.lower.01","editorial":"This lower comment quotes the short ruled note on I:247. Both printed occurrences are retained, not silently deduplicated.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I248.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":248,"physical_pdf_page":261,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وكالزاوية الخ) أعاد كاف التمثيل للاهتمام به للاختلاف الواقع في تقديرها اه منه","english":"On “Another example is the angle, etc.” He repeats the illustrative “such as” to draw attention to it, because of the disagreement over its determination. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I248.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I248.upper.01","volume":1,"page":248,"physical_pdf_page":261,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"أن اللون من خواص السطح ومعنى كون الجسم ملونا أنه سطحه ملون ولا تنافي بين كون كيفية محسوسة وكونها مختصة بالكم فإن قيل لو اعتبر التركيب لكان هناك أقسام لا تتناهى مع أنهم لم يعتدوا بها ولم يعدوها أنواعا قلنا لوجود لاجتماع اللون والشكل خصوصية بها اتصاف الجسم بالحسن والقبح عدوه نوعا على حدة وفي شرح المقاصد أن كلامهم متردد في أن الخلقة مجموع الشكل واللون أو كيفية حاصلة من اجتماعهما وهذا أقرب إلى جعلها نوعا على حدة","english":"—colour is a property of surface: to call a body coloured means that its surface is coloured. There is no incompatibility between a quality being sensible and its being proper to quantity. If it is objected that, if combination is taken into account, the divisions become infinite, whereas they neither consider all such combinations nor count them as species, we reply: the combination of colour and shape has a distinctive character through which the body is described as beautiful or ugly; they therefore count it as a separate species. In Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid it is stated that their wording oscillates between configuration’s being the combination of shape and colour and its being a quality resulting from their combination. The latter is closer to making configuration a separate species.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I248.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I247.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I248.upper.02","volume":1,"page":248,"physical_pdf_page":261,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وكالزاوية وهي هيئة إحاطة الخطين بالسطح) العارضة تلك الهيئة (عند الملتقى) أي ملتقى الخطين من غير أن يتحدا خطا واحدا فإنه إن اتصل خطان على نقطة","english":"“Another example is the angle: the configuration of two lines bounding a surface,” this configuration occurring “at their meeting,” that is, where the two lines meet without forming one line. For if two lines join at a point—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I248.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I249.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I249.lower.01","volume":1,"page":249,"physical_pdf_page":262,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"انتهى وكتب أعاد كاف التمثيل للاهتمام به للاختلاف الواقع فيها انتهى (أقول) الظاهر أن الزاوية لما كانت من أقسام الشكل كما مر والشكل معدود من المفردات دون المركبات أعاد الكاف عليها إشارة إلى مغايرتها للخلقة في الانفراد والتركيب لكن بعكس مغايرة الخلقة لما قبلها وأما تأخيرها عن نظائرها المفردة فلطول الكلام فيها فتبصر","english":"—end of quotation. He also wrote: “He repeats the illustrative ‘such as’ to draw attention to it, because of the disagreement concerning it.” End. I say: since an angle is a kind of shape, as above, and shape is counted among simple rather than composite qualities, he apparently repeats “such as” to indicate the angle’s difference from configuration in simplicity and composition—but with the relationship reversed from configuration’s difference from the preceding examples. Its postponement after the other simple examples is due to the length of its discussion. Consider attentively.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I249.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I248.lower.04","proof_notes":["Lower magnification resolves the final injunction فتبصر; uncertainty removed.","Complete upper and lower regions reread across overlapping enlargements; incoming/outgoing note continuations remain explicitly attached."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I249.lower.02","volume":1,"page":249,"physical_pdf_page":262,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فظن من هذا أنها الخ) يستفاد منه أن ظنهم هذا متفرع عن التفسير بالسطح المذكور مع أن الأمر بالعكس حيث قال في شرح المقاصد ذهب بعضهم إلى أن الزاوية من الكميات لكونها قابلة للقسمة بالذات ففسروها بسطح أحاط به خطان الخ انتهى (قوله إذ الزاوية الخ) حاصله المعارضة بأن ما ذكرتم من قبول القسمة وإن دل على مدعاكم لكن عندنا ما ينفيه (قوله إذ الزاوية الخ) وقد يجاب بأنا لا نسلم أن قبولها للقسمة بالذات بل بواسطة معروضها الذي هو السطح","english":"On “From this it was supposed that it, etc.” This makes their supposition a consequence of the above definition by surface. Yet the order is the reverse. In Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid he says: “Some held an angle to be a quantity because it is essentially divisible, and consequently defined it as a surface bounded by two lines, etc.” End. On “For an angle, etc.” The answer is a counterargument: even if the divisibility you mention supports your contention, we possess evidence against it. On the same phrase, another answer is possible: we do not grant that its divisibility is essential; it is divisible through its subject, the surface.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I249.jpg","stage":69,"proof_notes":["Complete upper and lower regions reread across overlapping enlargements; incoming/outgoing note continuations remain explicitly attached."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I249.lower.03","volume":1,"page":249,"physical_pdf_page":262,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كما يريدون بالشكل المشكل) زعم البعض من تقسيم الشكل إلى الدائرة والمثلث وغيرهما ثم تفسير الدائرة بأنها سطح يحيط به خط في وسطه نقطة الخ وتفسير المثلث بأنه سطح أحاط به ثلاثة خطوط أن الشكل هو نفس السطح فيكون كما والجمهور على أنه هو الهيئة الحاصلة من إحاطة الحد أو الحدود كما مر فيكون كيفا لا سطحا وتفسيره بالسطح فيها","english":"On “Just as they use ‘shape’ for the shaped thing.” Some inferred from the division of shape into circle, triangle and other forms, and from defining a circle as a surface enclosed by a line with a point in its centre, etc., and a triangle as a surface enclosed by three lines, that shape is the surface itself and therefore a quantity. The majority hold it to be the configuration resulting from enclosure by a boundary or boundaries, as above: it is a quality, not a surface. Defining it in these examples by surface—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I249.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I250.lower.01","proof_notes":["Complete upper and lower regions reread across overlapping enlargements; incoming/outgoing note continuations remain explicitly attached."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I249.upper.01","volume":1,"page":249,"physical_pdf_page":262,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"في سطح من غير أن يتحدا كذلك عرض لذلك السطح عند ملتقاهما هيئة انحدابية فيما بين الخطين المتصلين هي الزاوية (وقيل إنها سطح أحاط به خطان ملتقيان عند نقطة) من غير أن يتحدا كذلك والمراد أنها ما يلي تلك النقطة من السطح فظن من هذا أنها من الكميات وليس كذلك إذ الزاوية قد تنعدم بالزيادة بخلاف الكم (ففيه تسامح) مبني على أنهم يريدون بالزاوية ذا الزاوية كما يريدون بالشكل المشكل فيقولون مثلا المثلث شكل يحيط به ثلاثة أضلاع","english":"—on a surface without uniting in that manner, a configuration of inclination occurs in that surface between the two joining lines at their meeting: this is the angle. “It is also said to be a surface bounded by two lines meeting at a point,” without their uniting in that manner. What is meant is the portion of surface adjacent to that point. From this it was supposed that an angle is a quantity. It is not: an angle can disappear through increase, unlike quantity. “The expression is therefore loose.” It rests on their using “angle” for what has an angle, just as they use “shape” for the shaped thing. Thus they say, for example: a triangle is a shape bounded by three sides.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I249.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I248.upper.02","proof_notes":["Upper magnification clearly reads بالزيادة, “through increase,” not بالإزالة. The source contrasts the disappearance of a particular angle with the persistence of quantity under increase.","Complete upper and lower regions reread across overlapping enlargements; incoming/outgoing note continuations remain explicitly attached."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I249.upper.02","volume":1,"page":249,"physical_pdf_page":262,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الرابع من الأقسام (الكيفيات الاستعدادية) التي هي من جنس الاستعداد كما قال (وهي استعداد شديد على أن ينفعل) أي تهيؤ لقبول أثر ما بسهولة وسرعة (ويسمى ضعفا) ولا قوة (كالممراضية) واللين (أو) على أن (لا ينفعل)","english":"The fourth division comprises “dispositional qualities,” which belong to the genus of preparedness. As he says: “They are an intense preparedness to be affected,” that is, readiness to receive an effect easily and quickly, “called weakness” and lack of strength, “such as susceptibility to disease” and softness; “or” preparedness “not to be affected”—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I249.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I250.upper.01","proof_notes":["Complete upper and lower regions reread across overlapping enlargements; incoming/outgoing note continuations remain explicitly attached."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I250.lower.01","volume":1,"page":250,"physical_pdf_page":263,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"إنما هو لأنهم أرادوا بالشكل فيه المشكل إذ الشكل على معناه الحقيقي أعني الهيئة المذكورة إنما ينقسم إلى الاستدارة والتربيع والتثليث دون ما ذكر فإن قيل كيف يكون كيفا والنسبة مأخوذة في مفهومه قلنا لا نسلم ذلك إنما يتم لو كان المذكور في تفسيره حدا حقيقيا وما يقال من أنه من قبيل الوضع لأن هيئته حاصلة من نسبة أجزاء الجسم بعضها إلى بعض مردود بأن الحدود عبارة عن النهايات والانقطاعات كما مر فليست أجزاء للجسم ولا لغيره فافهم","english":"—is only because “shape” there means the shaped thing. In its true meaning—the configuration already mentioned—shape is divided into circularity, squareness and triangularity, not into those [figures]. If it is asked how it can be a quality when relation enters its concept, we reply: that is not conceded. The inference would follow only if the stated explanation were a real definition. The assertion that it belongs to position, since its configuration results from the mutual relation of bodily parts, is rejected: boundaries are endpoints and terminations, as above, not parts of the body or of anything else. Understand.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I250.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I249.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I250.lower.02","volume":1,"page":250,"physical_pdf_page":263,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ويسمى قوة الخ) إن قيل قد مر أن القوة قد تقال على صفة بها يتمكن الحيوان من مزاولة أفعال شاقة وقد تقال لمبدأ التأثير كالحرارة فإنها مبدأ الإحراق والأولى من الكيفية النفسانية والثانية من الكيفيات المحسوسة فكيف تعد من الكيفيات الاستعدادية قلنا الأصح كما مر هو أن أقسام الكيفية ليست متنافية بالذات بحيث يمتنع صدق البعض منها على شيء ما صدق عليه الآخر بل يجوز أن تعد القدرة من حيث اختصاصها بذوات الأنفس من الكيفيات النفسانية والحرارة من حيث كونها مدركة بالحس من المحسوسات وكل منهما من حيث كونها قوة شديدة تامة من الكيفيات الاستعدادية كما أن الاستقامة والانحناء ونحوهما مختصة بالكميات مع كونها من المحسوسات","english":"On “It is called strength, etc.” An objection: it was previously stated that “power” may signify a quality enabling an animal to perform arduous acts, or an originating principle of an effect—for example, heat, which initiates burning. The first is a psychic quality and the second a sensible quality. How, then, can power be included among dispositional qualities? We reply: as stated above, the sounder view is that the divisions of quality are not essentially exclusive, such that one cannot apply where another applies. Capacity can therefore be counted among psychic qualities insofar as it is proper to ensouled beings; heat among sensible qualities insofar as it is perceived by sense; and each among dispositional qualities insofar as it is an intense and complete power. Likewise straightness, curvature and similar qualities are proper to quantities while also being sensible.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I250.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I250.upper.01","volume":1,"page":250,"physical_pdf_page":263,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ويقاوم أي تهيؤ للمقاومة وبطء الانفعال (ويسمى قوة) ولا ضعفا (كالمصحاحية) والصلابة وهي الكيفية التي بها صار الجسم بحيث لا يقبل المرض","english":"—and to resist: readiness for resistance and for being affected only slowly. “This is called strength” and absence of weakness, “such as resistance to disease” and hardness. The former is the quality by which a body is such that it does not succumb to disease.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I250.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I249.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I250.upper.02","volume":1,"page":250,"physical_pdf_page":263,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(فصل في الأين) أثبت الحكماء المقولات النسبية وأنكرها المتكلمون إلا الأين ولذا قدمه على سائرها لكثرة مباحثه (وهو) عند المتكلمين (الكون) أي الحصول (في الحيز) ثم إنهم حصروه في أربعة أنواع الاجتماع والافتراق والحركة والسكون (فإن اعتبر حصول جوهر) في حيز (باعتبار جوهر آخر) فأما أن يمكن تخلل جوهر (ثالث بينهما فافتراق) وإنما اعتبر إمكان توسط ثالث ليتناول افتراق الجوهرين بتوسط الخلاء (وإلا) يمكن ذلك (فاجتماع وإن لم يعتبر) حصول جوهر في حيز بالنسبة إلى آخر (فإن كان)","english":"“Section on where [ayn].” The philosophers affirm the relational categories, while the theologians deny them except for “where.” He therefore treats it before the others, because its investigations are numerous. “It is,” according to the theologians, “being”—that is, being present—“in a spatial region.” They restrict it to four kinds: conjunction, separation, motion and rest. “If the presence of a substance” in a spatial region “is considered with reference to another substance,” then, if “a third” substance can intervene “between them, it is separation.” The possibility of a third’s intervening is specified so as to include two substances separated by a void. “Otherwise”—if such intervention is impossible—“it is conjunction. If” the presence of a substance in a spatial region “is not considered” relative to another, “then, if it is—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I250.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I251.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I251.lower.01","volume":1,"page":251,"physical_pdf_page":264,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أيضا كذا قالوا (قوله أو كان مسبوقا به في حيز الخ) فيكون السكون حصولا ثانيا في حيز أول والحركة حصولا أول في حيز ثان لكن أولية الحيز في السكون لا يلزم أن تكون تحقيقا بل تكون تقديرا كما في الساكن الذي لا يتحرك أصلا ولا يحصل في حيز ثان وكذا أولية الحصول في الحركة قد تكون تقديرا لجواز أن ينعدم المتحرك في آن انقطاع الحركة فلا يتحقق له حصول ثان فإن قيل إذا اعتبر في الحركة المسبوقية بالحصول في حيز آخر لم يكن الخروج من الحيز الأول حركة مع أنه حركة وفاقا أجيب بأنه إنما يلزم ذلك لو لم يكن ذلك الخروج نفس الحصول الأول في الحيز الثاني والحق أنه نفسه فإن الحصول الأول في الحيز الثاني من حيث الإضافة إليه دخول وحركة إليه ومن حيث الإضافة إلى الحيز الأول خروج وحركة منه","english":"—also. Thus they have said. On “Or it is preceded by presence in another region, etc.” Rest is therefore a second presence in a first region, while motion is a first presence in a second region. Yet the region’s being “first” in rest need not be actual: it may be hypothetical, as in something at rest that never moves or occupies a second region. Likewise, the presence’s being “first” in motion may be hypothetical, since the moving thing may cease to exist at the instant when motion ends, never acquiring a second presence. If it is objected that requiring motion to be preceded by presence in another region excludes departure from the first region, though departure is admittedly motion, the reply is that this follows only if the departure is not itself the first presence in the second region. In fact, it is that very presence: relative to the second region it is entry and motion toward it; relative to the first it is departure and motion away from it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I251.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I250.lower.02","editorial":"The opening أيضا كذا قالوا closes the preceding marginal discussion of qualities. The unnumbered end marker after this argument is not treated as an author name.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I251.lower.02","volume":1,"page":251,"physical_pdf_page":264,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ليس بحركة ولا سكون الخ) أما أنه ليس باجتماع ولا افتراق فلأنه فرض انفراده وأما أنه ليس بحركة ولا سكون فلأن حصوله في آن الحدوث ليس مسبوقا بحصول آخر لا في ذلك الحيز ولا في حيز آخر","english":"On “It is neither motion nor rest, etc.” It is neither conjunction nor separation because its isolation was stipulated. It is neither motion nor rest because its presence at the instant of origination is not preceded by another presence, either in the same region or in another.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I251.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I251.lower.03","volume":1,"page":251,"physical_pdf_page":264,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لكونه مماثلا للحصول الثاني الخ) وإليه ذهب جمع قالوا حقيقة الأكوان هو الحصول في الحيز واللبث ونحوه من الأمور الزائدة الخارجة عن الحقيقة وحينئذ لا يتم ما ذكر في طريق الحصر بل طريقه أن يقال إن الحصول في الحيز إن كان مسبوقا بحصوله في حيز آخر فحركة وإلا فسكون ليدخل الحصول آن الحدوث في السكون وأورد عليه أنه وإن دخل فيه ذلك لكن","english":"On “Because it resembles the second presence, etc.” A group adopts this view. They say that the reality of spatial modes is presence in a region; remaining there and similar matters are additions outside that reality. The proposed exhaustive division therefore fails. It should instead be stated: presence in a region is motion if preceded by presence in another region, and otherwise rest. Presence at the instant of origination is thereby included in rest. The objection is that, although this includes it, nevertheless—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I251.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I252.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I251.upper.01","volume":1,"page":251,"physical_pdf_page":264,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"مسبوقا بحصوله في ذلك الحيز فسكون أو) كان مسبوقا به (في) حيز (آخر فحركة) وعلم من تقييدهم الحصول بالحيز أنهم لا يثبتون الحركة في سائر المقولات وعلى ما ذكر من بيان الأكوان الأربعة لو فرضنا أنه تعالى خلق جوهرا فردا ولم يخلق معه آخر (فالحصول) لذلك الجوهر (في آن الحدوث خارج) ليس بحركة ولا سكون ولا اجتماع ولا افتراق (وقيل بل سكون) لكونه مماثلا للحصول الثاني في ذلك الحيز لاشتراكهما في كون كل منهما موجبا للاختصاص بذلك الحيز وهو سكون بالاتفاق واللبث أمر زائد غير مشروط فيه ورد بمنع التماثل","english":"—“preceded by its presence in that same spatial region, it is rest; or,” if preceded by its presence “in another” region, “it is motion.” Their qualification of presence by spatial region shows that they do not affirm motion in the other categories. On this account of the four modes of spatial being, suppose that God creates a single indivisible substance and creates no other alongside it. “Its presence at the instant of origination falls outside” the four: it is neither motion, rest, conjunction nor separation. “It has also been said to be rest,” because it resembles the second presence in that region: both entail specification to that region, and the second is rest by agreement. Remaining there is an additional matter, not a condition of rest. This is answered by denying the resemblance—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I251.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I250.upper.02","join_out":"I252.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I252.lower.01","volume":1,"page":252,"physical_pdf_page":265,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"يخرج منه السكون بعد الحركة إذ يصدق عليه أنه حصول مسبوق بالحصول في حيز آخر فيكون حركة فالأولى أن يقال إنه إن اتصل بحصول سابق في حيز آخر فحركة وإلا فسكون فيدخل الحصول آن الحدوث والسكون بعد الحركة لكن يخرج الأكوان المتلاحقة في الأحياز المتلاصقة أعني التي هي أجزاء الحركة فلا يصح ما قالوا إن الحركة مجموع سكنات فظهر أن اعتبار عدم الاتصال بالحصول في حيز آخر مصحح إدخال الحصول آن الحدوث في السكون ولكن هذا الذي ذكرته فإنه نافع فيما يأتي مناقشة قالوا كون أجزاء الحركة سكنات مبني على ما ذهب إليه البعض من أن الحصول الأول في الحيز الثاني مماثل للحصول الثاني فيه وهو سكون اتفاقا فكذا الأول فيكون هذا إلزاما لمن قال بتماثل الحصول الثاني في الحيز الأول ثم اعترض","english":"—rest after motion is excluded, since it too is presence preceded by presence in another region, and would consequently be motion. It is therefore preferable to say: if a presence is immediately connected with an earlier presence in another region, it is motion; otherwise it is rest. Presence at origination and rest after motion are thereby included. But the successive modes in contiguous regions—the parts of motion—are excluded [from rest], so their statement that motion is a collection of rests cannot stand. Requiring absence of immediate connection with presence in another region thus makes it possible to include presence at origination in rest. Yet there is a qualification to what I have said, useful for what follows. They say: the claim that the parts of motion are rests rests upon the view of some that the first presence in a second region resembles the second presence there. The latter is rest by agreement; so too, then, is the former. This is consequently an objection directed against whoever affirms resemblance to the second presence in the first region. It was then objected—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I252.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I251.lower.03","join_out":"I253.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"ولكن هذا الذي ذكرته فإنه نافع فيما يأتي مناقشة","status":"The compressed linking sentence is insecure; punctuation and rendering are provisional."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I252.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":252,"physical_pdf_page":265,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فيصدق على الأول الخ) يعني الدليل المذكور للقول بأن الحصول في آن الحدوث سكون جار في الحصول الأول في الحيز الثاني فيكون هو أيضا سكونا فيرد عليه أن الحصول في الحيز الثاني الخ فحينئذ أجيب عنه بأن عدم الخ فالناقل ورد هذا الجواب بكذا الخ اه منه","english":"On “It applies to the first, etc.” That is, the argument for calling presence at the instant of origination rest also applies to the first presence in a second region, making that rest as well. The objection concerning presence in the second region is then raised, and answered by the condition of not being preceded, etc. The reporter rejects this answer in the manner stated. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I252.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"فالناقل ورد","status":"The linking words near the end are unclear in the scan; the argument is rendered provisionally without claiming a secure transcription."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I252.ruled.02","volume":1,"page":252,"physical_pdf_page":265,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فيصدق على الثاني دون الأول) وهذا وإن كان صادقا على الحصول في آن الحدوث لكن من قال بأنه خارج قال باشتراط اللبث في السكون اه منه","english":"On “It applies to the second, not the first.” Although this also applies to presence at the instant of origination, those who exclude that presence [from the fourfold division] require remaining in a region for rest. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I252.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I252.upper.01","volume":1,"page":252,"physical_pdf_page":265,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"والاشتراك المذكور ليس أخص صفاتهما كيف والحصول الأول في الحيز الثاني حركة وفاقا ولو كان مماثلا للحصول الثاني فيه لزم أن يكون هو أيضا حركة ولا قائل به فإن أجيب بأن عدم المسبوقية بالحصول في ذلك الحيز معتبر في الحركة فيصدق على الأول دون الثاني قلنا فكذلك عدم الاتصال بالحصول في حيز آخر معتبر في السكون فيصدق على الثاني دون الأول ثم إنه اختلف في أن التمايز والتغاير بين الأكوان ذاتي أو اعتباري (والحق أن حقيقة الكون) وهو الحصول في الحيز (في الكل واحدة وإنما التمايز بالحيثيات) والاعتبارات لا الفصول المنوعة","english":"—and by saying that the common feature mentioned is not their most specific property. How could it be? The first presence in a second region is motion by agreement. If it were like the second presence there, the latter would also have to be motion, which no one maintains. If it is answered that motion requires not being preceded by presence in that same region, so that it applies to the first presence but not the second, we reply: likewise rest requires not being immediately connected with presence in another region; it therefore applies to the second presence but not the first. There is also disagreement over whether the distinction and difference between spatial modes is intrinsic or conceptual. “The truth is that the reality of spatial being”—presence in a region—“is one in all of them. Their distinction is only through aspects” and considerations, not through differentiae constituting distinct species.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I252.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I251.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I253.lower.01","volume":1,"page":253,"physical_pdf_page":266,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"عليه الآمدي بما ذكره الشارح بقوله ورد بمنع التماثل الخ وحاصله أنا لا نسلم تماثل الحصولين في الحيز الثاني وإلا لزم القول بكون الحصول الثاني فيه حركة كما أن الحصول الأول فيه حركة اتفاقا ولا قائل به فإن أجيب عن هذا المنع بأنه يمكن أن يقال إن الحركة قد أخذ فيها عدم المسبوقية بالحصول في ذلك الحيز فهي تصدق على الحصول الأول في الحيز الثاني دون الثاني فيه تقول يمكن أن يعتبر في السكون عدم الاتصال بحيز آخر فيصدق على الحصول الثاني في الحيز الثاني دون الأول فيه فلا يتم القول بكون الحصول الذي هو جزء الحركة سكونا لأنه حصول أول في الأحياز المتلاحقة فظهر أن قول الشارح «مد ظله» فإن أجيب بأن عدم المسبوقية إلى قوله قلنا الخ هو هذا الذي قلناه وهو بحث متعلق بالأكوان التي هي أجزاء الحركة التي جعلت سكنات وليس متعلقا بالكون الذي هو في آن الحدوث كما يصرح به صنيع الشارح «مد ظله» فإنه غير مستقيم إذ اعتبار عدم الاتصال بالحصول في حيز آخر إنما يصير نقضا على عد الكون الذي هو جزء الحركة من السكنات لا على عد السكون في آن الحدوث سكونا بل هو مصحح لذلك كما سبق آنفا فليتأمل","english":"—by al-Āmidī, with the argument the commentator reports in “It is answered by denying the resemblance, etc.” Its substance is: we do not concede that the two presences in the second region resemble each other. Otherwise the second presence there would have to be motion, just as the first is motion by agreement, although no one says this. If this denial is answered by saying that motion includes the condition of not being preceded by presence in that region, so that motion applies to the first presence in the second region but not the second, we say: rest may likewise include the condition of not being immediately connected with another region. It then applies to the second presence in the second region, not the first. The claim that the presence forming part of motion is rest therefore fails, since it is a first presence in each successive region. The commentator’s words—may his protection endure—from “If it is answered that not being preceded” to “we reply,” are thus the argument we have just given. They concern the spatial modes that constitute motion and have been called rests, not spatial being at the instant of origination, as the commentator’s treatment suggests. That treatment is not sound: requiring absence of immediate connection with presence in another region refutes calling a part of motion rest, not calling the initial state at origination rest. Indeed, it supports the latter, as just explained. Let this be considered.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I253.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I252.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"stage81_same_editor_visual_review":"whole_visible_page_second_source_inspection_unresolved_glyphs_not_externally_certified","stage81_register_audit":"one upper line plus three lower divisions","stage81_boundary_audit":"I:252 upper -> I:253 upper -> I:254 upper; lower gloss continuous","stage81_proof_note":"Motion / rest distinction and spatial first presence preserved; source text reviewed for first-vs-second occurrence of الحصول."},{"id":"I253.lower.02","volume":1,"page":253,"physical_pdf_page":266,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"إن قيل إن الحركة ضد السكون فكيف تكون نفسه أو مركبة منه أجيب بأن التضاد ليس بين الحركة إلى الحيز والسكون فيه بل لا تغاير بينهما وإنما التضاد بين الحركة من الحيز والسكون فيه كما يصرح به وقد مرت الإشارة إليه فتدبر","english":"If it is objected that motion is contrary to rest, so how can it be identical with or composed of rest, the reply is: the contrariety is not between motion toward a region and rest in it; there is no difference between those. It is between motion away from a region and rest in it, as he expressly states. This has already been indicated. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I253.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"stage81_same_editor_visual_review":"whole_visible_page_second_source_inspection_unresolved_glyphs_not_externally_certified","stage81_register_audit":"one upper line plus three lower divisions","stage81_boundary_audit":"I:252 upper -> I:253 upper -> I:254 upper; lower gloss continuous","stage81_proof_note":"Motion / rest distinction and spatial first presence preserved; source text reviewed for first-vs-second occurrence of الحصول."},{"id":"I253.lower.03","volume":1,"page":253,"physical_pdf_page":266,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولا في الهوية الشخصية الخ) والحاصل أن إطلاق الأنواع على الأكوان مجاز لأن حقيقتها الحصول في الحيز والأمور المميزة لها خارجة عنه حاصلة باختلاف الإضافات والاعتبارات فتغايرها بتلك الإضافات لا يوجب الاختلاف في الماهية بل ربما لا يوجب تعدد أشخاصها أيضا لكن قد يوجب تغايرها في الخارج كالحركة من حيز والسكون فيه فيئذ يقال بتضادهما يعني مجرد امتناع اجتماعهما في الوجود ولو من جهة التماثل فإن المتماثلين إذا تغايرا في الخارج امتنع اجتماعهما لا بمعنى امتناع الاجتماع من جهة الاختلاف بالذات كما هو ظاهر فظهر أن الأولى أن يقول بدل قوله ولا في الهوية الخ بل ربما لا يوجب","english":"On “Nor even in individual identity, etc.” In sum, calling the spatial modes “species” is figurative. Their reality is presence in a region; the differentiating features lie outside it and arise from different relations and considerations. Their difference through those relations does not entail a difference of quiddity and may not even require numerically distinct instances. It may nevertheless entail external difference, as with motion away from a region and rest in it. In that case they are called contraries, meaning merely that they cannot coexist, even if this is due to their similarity: externally distinct instances of the same kind cannot coincide. It does not mean impossibility of coexistence due to intrinsic difference. This is clear. It is therefore preferable to replace “Nor even in individual identity, etc.” with “Indeed, it may not require—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I253.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I254.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"stage81_same_editor_visual_review":"whole_visible_page_second_source_inspection_unresolved_glyphs_not_externally_certified","stage81_register_audit":"one upper line plus three lower divisions","stage81_boundary_audit":"I:252 upper -> I:253 upper -> I:254 upper; lower gloss continuous","stage81_proof_note":"Motion / rest distinction and spatial first presence preserved; source text reviewed for first-vs-second occurrence of الحصول."},{"id":"I253.upper.01","volume":1,"page":253,"physical_pdf_page":266,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ولا يوجب ذلك اختلافا في الماهية بل ولا في الهوية الشخصية (حتى إن)","english":"This does not entail difference in quiddity, or even in individual identity, “so that—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I253.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I254.upper.01","editorial":"This is the whole principal-text span on I:253. The remainder is the separate lower commentary.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"stage81_same_editor_visual_review":"whole_visible_page_second_source_inspection_unresolved_glyphs_not_externally_certified","stage81_register_audit":"one upper line plus three lower divisions","stage81_boundary_audit":"I:252 upper -> I:253 upper -> I:254 upper; lower gloss continuous","stage81_proof_note":"Motion / rest distinction and spatial first presence preserved; source text reviewed for first-vs-second occurrence of الحصول."},{"id":"I254.lower.01","volume":1,"page":254,"physical_pdf_page":267,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"تعدد الهوية فافهم (قوله أي قد يطلق لفظ الحركة الخ) يريد بيان أن الحكماء لما أطلقوا الحركة على معنيين أحدهما محقق موجود عندهم والآخر موهوم كما سيأتي أطلقها المتكلمون على معنيين أيضا أحدهما بمقابلة المعنى الأول وهو الحصول في حيز بعد الحصول في آخر ويسمونه بالإضافة إلى الحيز السابق خروجا","english":"—multiplicity of individual identity.” Understand. On “That is, the term motion may be used, etc.” He intends to explain that, just as the philosophers use “motion” in two senses, one actual and existent according to them and the other imagined, as will follow, the theologians also use it in two senses. One corresponds to the first meaning: presence in a region after presence in another. Relative to the preceding region, they call it departure—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I254.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I253.lower.03","join_out":"I255.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I254.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":254,"physical_pdf_page":267,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وكما في الحركة والسكون الخ) بأن الحركة من حيز مع السكون فيه هذا (خ) وأما الحركة إلى حيز مع السكون فيه فيجتمعان إن لم يشترط اللبث فيه لعدم التمايز بينهما إلا بالحيثية اه منه «مد ظله»","english":"On “The same applies to motion and rest, etc.” This means motion away from a region together with rest in it. As for motion toward a region together with rest in it, they coincide if remaining is not required for rest, since they differ only in aspect. End. [Signed:] “From him, may his protection endure.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I254.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"A small parenthetic mark after هذا is retained in Arabic without assigning it an interpretative meaning.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I254.upper.01","volume":1,"page":254,"physical_pdf_page":267,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الكون (الواحد بالشخص ربما يكون افتراقا) بالنسبة إلى جوهر (واجتماعا) بالنسبة إلى آخر (وحركة) من جهة كونه مسبوقا بحصول في حيز آخر (وسكونا) من جهة بقائه في الزمان الثاني (باعتبارات مختلفة) كما ذكر فلم يختلف بها نفس الكون والحصول أصلا وإن اختلف بها وصف كونه افتراقا واجتماعا وحركة وسكونا","english":"—“one numerically identical” spatial mode “may be separation” relative to one substance, “conjunction” relative to another, “motion” insofar as it is preceded by presence in another region, “and rest” insofar as it persists into the second time, “under different considerations,” as explained. Spatial being and presence themselves do not thereby differ at all, although their descriptions as separation, conjunction, motion and rest do differ.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I254.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I253.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I254.upper.02","volume":1,"page":254,"physical_pdf_page":267,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والقول بتضاد الأكوان) الأربعة مع القول باتحاد الحقيقة ليس (معناه) امتناع الاجتماع بالذات المعتبر في التضاد بل مجرد (امتناع الاجتماع عند تغيرها في الوجود) كما في اجتماع جوهر في حيز مع جوهر معين فإنه لا يجامع افتراقه عنه بعينه في ذلك الحيز وكما في الحركة والسكون بالنسبة إلى حيز واحد","english":"“The assertion that the four spatial modes are contrary,” together with the assertion that their reality is one, “does not mean” the intrinsic impossibility of coexistence required for contrariety, but merely “the impossibility of their coexisting when they differ in existence.” Thus a substance’s conjunction in a region with a specified substance cannot coexist with its separation from that very substance in that region. The same applies to motion and rest relative to one region.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I254.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"عند تغيرها","status":"Likely تغايرها (“their differing”), to be checked at native resolution; not a change-process claim."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I254.upper.03","volume":1,"page":254,"physical_pdf_page":267,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(واعلم أن الحركة قد يراد بها) أي قد يطلق لفظ الحركة ويراد بها (ما هو المحقق منها وهو الحصول) في حيز (بعد الحصول في حيز آخر) وقد تطلق (ويراد بها ما هو الموهوم) منها (وهو الحصولات المتعاقبة) في أحياز متلاصقة (على الاستمرار) في تلك الأحياز (دون الاستقرار) في واحد منها (والسكون إن لم يشترط اللبث) كما ذهب إليه البعض (فالحركة) في الحيز (سكون) واحد","english":"“Know that motion may mean”—that is, the term may be used for—“what is actual in it: presence” in one region “after presence in another.” Alternatively, it may mean “what is imagined in it: successive presences” in contiguous regions, “continuously,” passing through those regions “without settling” in any one. “If rest does not require remaining”—as some maintain—“then motion” in a region “is one rest—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I254.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I255.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I255.lower.01","volume":1,"page":255,"physical_pdf_page":268,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"واللاحق دخولا والآخر بمقابلة المعنى الثاني وهو الحصولات متعاقبة (قوله المعنى الأول لها الخ) أي للحركة عند المتكلمين وكذا يكون الحصول آن الحدوث حينئذ سكونا كما مر (قوله لا بين الحركة في الحيز الخ) إذ كل حركة في الحيز سكون عندهم لكن لا عكس كما فهم مما سبق فافهم","english":"—and relative to the subsequent region, entry. The other sense corresponds to the second meaning: successive presences. On “According to its first meaning, etc.”: the first meaning of motion among the theologians. Presence at the instant of origination is likewise rest on that account, as above. On “Not between motion within the region, etc.” Every motion within a region is rest according to them, but not conversely, as is understood from the preceding discussion. Understand.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I255.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I254.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I255.lower.02","volume":1,"page":255,"physical_pdf_page":268,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أي السكون الخ) أي عند القائلين باشتراط اللبث فيه إذ الاختلاف في أنه مجموع الحصولين أو أحدهما إنما يتأتى على القول بهذا الاشتراط كما هو واضح فلا يكون الحركة عند مشترطي اللبث ولا أجزاؤها ولا الحصول في آن الحدوث سكونا كما صرح به في شرح المقاصد","english":"On “That is, rest, etc.”: according to those who require remaining in a region. The disagreement over whether rest is both presences or one of them arises only if this condition is imposed, as is evident. Those who require remaining therefore do not count motion, its parts, or presence at origination as rest, as Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid explicitly states.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I255.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I255.lower.03","volume":1,"page":255,"physical_pdf_page":268,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كما قد يحمل قولهم الحركة الخ) أقول إطلاق قولهم إن الحركة قد يراد بها ما هو المحقق منها الخ صريح في أن مرادهم بالحركة هو أحد الحصولين لا مجموعهما في الحيزين ضرورة أن المجموع موهوم كالمعنى الثاني للحركة وكذا إطلاق قولهم إن الحركة عند من لم يشترط اللبث في السكون إما سكون واحد","english":"On “Just as their statement concerning motion may be taken, etc.” I say: the unqualified assertion that motion may mean what is actual in it explicitly indicates that motion means one of the two presences, not their combination in two regions. The combination is necessarily imagined, like the second meaning of motion. Likewise, their unqualified assertion that motion, according to those who do not require remaining for rest, is either one rest—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I255.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I256.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I255.upper.01","volume":1,"page":255,"physical_pdf_page":268,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فيه بالمعنى الأول لها (أو) مجموع (سكنات) في أحياز متلاصقة بالمعنى الثاني الموهوم والتضاد إنما هو بين الحركة من الحيز والسكون فيه لا بين الحركة في الحيز والسكون فيه (وهل هو) أي السكون (الحصول الثاني) من الحصولين في حيز واحد (أو مجموع الحصولين فيه تردد) والظاهر من عبارة القسم هو الحصول الثاني منهما لكن الأقرب كما قال في شرح المقاصد أن المراد أنه مجموع الحصولين كما قد يحمل قولهم الحركة حصول في الحيز بعد الحصول في حيز آخر على أنها مجموع الحصولين","english":"—“in it,” according to the first meaning of motion, “or” a collection of “rests” in contiguous regions, according to its second, imagined meaning. Contrariety is only between motion away from a region and rest in it, not between motion within a region and rest in it. “Whether it”—rest—“is the second presence,” of two presences in one region, “or their combination is uncertain.” The wording of the division apparently means the second presence. Yet the closer interpretation, as stated in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, is that it means their combination, just as the statement “motion is presence in a region after presence in another” may be taken to mean the combination of the two presences.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I255.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I254.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I255.upper.02","volume":1,"page":255,"physical_pdf_page":268,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ثم إنهم اختلفوا في أمرين أنهما مما حركة أو سكون أحدهما حال الأجزاء الباطنة من الجسم المتحرك والآخر حال الجسم المستقر المتبدل المحدد به ولو سطحه كما يحيط به واختار المصنف أن الأول حركة والثاني سكون كما قال (والحق أن الباطن من أجزاء المتحرك متحرك) وأن (الواقف عند هبوب الرياح) كالطير الواقف عنده (و) الواقف (عند جريان الماء عليه)","english":"They then disagree over whether two cases are motion or rest. The first is the state of the inner parts of a moving body; the second, the state of a stationary body whose surrounding boundary changes, even if that boundary is a surface enclosing it. The author chooses motion in the first case and rest in the second: “The truth is that the inner parts of a moving thing move,” and that “what remains stationary while winds blow”—such as a bird holding its position—and what remains stationary “while water flows over it—”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I255.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I256.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"أنهما مما حركة / المحدد به ولو سطحه كما يحيط به","status":"Several tightly printed connecting words are uncertain. The two cases themselves are clear; wording must receive another visual proof."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I256.lower.01","volume":1,"page":256,"physical_pdf_page":269,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أو مجموع سكنات صريح في ذلك أيضا ضرورة أن مجموع الحصولين في الحيزين لا يكون سكونا واحدا إلا أن يقال إن الإطلاقين المذكورين رأي الأكثر والحمل المذكور رأي البعض ولا بعد فإن يكون إدراج لفظة قد للإشارة إلى هذا فتدبره فإنه دقيق ثم ما ذكر من الأقربية إن تم فلعله بالنظر إلى العرف فليتأمل","english":"—or a collection of rests is equally explicit, because the combination of two presences in two regions cannot be one rest. One could, however, say that the two unqualified formulations express the majority view and the interpretation proposed belongs to a minority. It is quite possible that “may” was inserted to indicate this. Consider it carefully: the point is subtle. If the claim that this interpretation is “closer” holds, perhaps it is closer in ordinary usage. Let this be considered.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I256.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I255.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I256.lower.02","volume":1,"page":256,"physical_pdf_page":269,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ومبنى التردد الخ) أي الخلاف من الفريقين لا حقيقة الشك فلا ينافي ما سبق من قوله والحق أن الباطن الخ فحقق","english":"On “The basis of the uncertainty, etc.” This means disagreement between the two parties, not genuine doubt. It therefore does not conflict with the earlier assertion “The truth is that the interior, etc.” Investigate this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I256.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false}]);